Can we load up the container box?

256
00:09:35,529 --> 00:09:36,629
Sure.

257
00:09:37,099 --> 00:09:38,998
- I see. You cover it up first. - Yes.

258
00:09:38,999 --> 00:09:40,679
(Covering it with a net before loading it)

259
00:09:40,799 --> 00:09:42,439
- That way, things won't fall off. - Yes.

260
00:09:43,739 --> 00:09:46,008
Hold on, sir!

261
00:09:46,009 --> 00:09:47,409
- You can pile that on. - Okay.

262
00:09:47,509 --> 00:09:49,179
- Right here. - Give me a bit more time.

263
00:09:49,409 --> 00:09:50,608
- What do you need to do? Come here. - Wait.

264
00:09:50,609 --> 00:09:51,969
Right here. I've got an opening here.

265
00:09:52,079 --> 00:09:53,208
- You do? - Come here.

266
00:09:53,209 --> 00:09:55,279
I wanted to get rid of the trash in a hurry.

267
00:09:56,079 --> 00:09:57,719
- We needed to load everything up. - Yes.

268
00:09:57,749 --> 00:09:58,989
Push everything in.

269
00:09:59,989 --> 00:10:01,018
- What's that? - I got it.

270
00:10:01,019 --> 00:10:02,659
- You're throwing out the ice box? - Yes.

271
00:10:02,859 --> 00:10:04,535
Like students who want to write down answers...

272
00:10:04,559 --> 00:10:06,199
- I'm sorry about this. - when time's up.

273
00:10:07,489 --> 00:10:09,298
(Finishing up like students cramming at the last minute)

274
00:10:09,299 --> 00:10:10,698
That's when you start spotting...

275
00:10:10,699 --> 00:10:12,129
- a lot of trash. - Yes.

276
00:10:12,369 --> 00:10:13,698
All the answers you put down now...

277
00:10:13,699 --> 00:10:15,139
- turn out to be correct too. - Yes.

278
00:10:16,599 --> 00:10:17,669
We're good now.

279
00:10:19,209 --> 00:10:21,039
My gosh, we got a lot of things done.

280
00:10:21,509 --> 00:10:23,138
Sir, how much weight can the container carry?

281
00:10:23,139 --> 00:10:24,979
The container can carry about 4t.

282
00:10:25,079 --> 00:10:26,079
About 4t?

283
00:10:26,349 --> 00:10:27,778
- My gosh, 4t. - We collected 4t of trash.

284
00:10:27,779 --> 00:10:29,018
- You'll probably need... - Let that go.

285
00:10:29,019 --> 00:10:30,279
Another container of this.

286
00:10:30,679 --> 00:10:31,918
- Right. - She'll need...

287
00:10:31,919 --> 00:10:33,189
- two more of that. - Yes.

288
00:10:34,419 --> 00:10:35,818
I'm a maximalist for a reason.

289
00:10:35,819 --> 00:10:36,918
My grandma was a bigger maximalist...

290
00:10:36,919 --> 00:10:38,018
- than me. - Seriously.

291
00:10:38,019 --> 00:10:41,289
But you guys helped me with the first step.

292
00:10:41,629 --> 00:10:43,759
The 1st step? Does it mean there's a 2nd step for us?

293
00:10:44,829 --> 00:10:45,928
Listen.

294
00:10:45,929 --> 00:10:48,099
- Call Ju Seung and Sung Hwan next. - Yes.

295
00:10:48,199 --> 00:10:50,168
- You'll end late at night then. - Yes.

296
00:10:50,169 --> 00:10:51,398
They're not good workers.

297
00:10:51,399 --> 00:10:52,938
You guys are the best workers.

298
00:10:52,939 --> 00:10:54,039
- Seriously. - Of course.

299
00:10:55,109 --> 00:10:56,278
(Thanks to them, they collected a lot of trash.)

300
00:10:56,279 --> 00:10:57,708
Gosh, we did so well.

301
00:10:57,709 --> 00:10:59,909
I can't believe we filled that up.

302
00:11:00,209 --> 00:11:01,649
- I can't believe this. - My gosh.

303
00:11:02,479 --> 00:11:04,119
Can he actually load that onto the truck?

304
00:11:05,749 --> 00:11:07,348
- The truck might topple over. - My gosh.

305
00:11:07,349 --> 00:11:08,719
(The 4t container starts tilting.)

306
00:11:09,219 --> 00:11:10,458
- The truck might topple over. - My gosh.

307
00:11:10,459 --> 00:11:11,828
(Is it going to be okay?)

308
00:11:11,829 --> 00:11:13,389
He struggled to load it onto the truck.

309
00:11:14,289 --> 00:11:15,599
- My gosh. - That was scary.

310
00:11:15,999 --> 00:11:17,629
(Spitting out)

311
00:11:18,529 --> 00:11:20,369
(He quickly grabs the bucket that falls out.)

312
00:11:20,799 --> 00:11:22,945
- Let's keep that here. - Let's throw it out next time.

313
00:11:22,969 --> 00:11:24,139
It was meant to stay.

314
00:11:25,039 --> 00:11:27,368
- Bye. - Thank you.

315
00:11:27,369 --> 00:11:28,808
- Thank you. - Bye.

316
00:11:28,809 --> 00:11:30,079
Can't I just throw this in?

317
00:11:30,309 --> 00:11:31,808
Gosh, how did we manage to do all this?

318
00:11:31,809 --> 00:11:33,408
Right. How did you guys fill that up?

319
00:11:33,409 --> 00:11:34,478
- This is just unbelievable. - My gosh.

320
00:11:34,479 --> 00:11:35,549
The three of you did it.

321
00:11:37,319 --> 00:11:41,148
(It's time to say goodbye at last.)

322
00:11:41,149 --> 00:11:42,919
- Gosh, it looks clean now. - Yes.

323
00:11:51,629 --> 00:11:52,729
Well done.

324
00:11:56,699 --> 00:11:58,609
It felt like getting my wisdom tooth out.

325
00:11:58,909 --> 00:12:01,008
I didn't need to get it out because it was rotten or anything.

326
00:12:01,009 --> 00:12:02,708
I knew I had to get it done.

327
00:12:02,709 --> 00:12:05,449
But I kept pushing it off. Then once I did it,

328
00:12:06,149 --> 00:12:08,278
I was glad I did it.

329
00:12:08,279 --> 00:12:10,279
It really gave me a liberating feeling.

330
00:12:13,389 --> 00:12:15,558
(As deep as her love for them was,)

331
00:12:15,559 --> 00:12:19,759
(the wisdom tooth was stuck just as painfully.)

332
00:12:21,229 --> 00:12:25,899
(She didn't have the courage to pull it out.)

333
00:12:27,229 --> 00:12:29,469
It really was the first step.

334
00:12:30,699 --> 00:12:32,338
- You guys did a great job. - You did well.

335
00:12:32,339 --> 00:12:34,609
- It looks the way it did before. - You did so well.

336
00:12:35,409 --> 00:12:39,079
(Turning it back just as it used to be)

337
00:12:39,349 --> 00:12:41,548
- Gosh, you guys did a great job. - Oh, my.

338
00:12:41,549 --> 00:12:43,079
It looks clean now.

339
00:12:43,319 --> 00:12:44,449
It's clean now.

340
00:12:45,389 --> 00:12:47,488
I couldn't have done it without their help.

341
00:12:47,489 --> 00:12:48,818
I couldn't have made it inside.

342
00:12:48,819 --> 00:12:51,059
Right. This is liberating.

343
00:12:51,889 --> 00:12:55,299
First of all, I pulled out something that wasn't a weed.

344
00:12:55,399 --> 00:12:56,798
I made a lot of mistakes too.

345
00:12:56,799 --> 00:12:58,329
But it was rewarding.

346
00:12:58,399 --> 00:13:00,868
It turned back to the yard I once recognized.

347
00:13:00,869 --> 00:13:02,339
It was a spacious yard.

348
00:13:02,539 --> 00:13:04,438
It gave me a liberating feeling...

349
00:13:04,439 --> 00:13:06,239
when I saw the yard was somewhat tidied up.

350
00:13:06,869 --> 00:13:08,869
- It was quite rewarding. - It was very rewarding.

351
00:13:08,979 --> 00:13:11,008
Even if it takes time,

352
00:13:11,009 --> 00:13:14,149
I'll come down and fix up the house little by little.

353
00:13:14,479 --> 00:13:16,679
I want my relatives and family to come to this house...

354
00:13:16,719 --> 00:13:19,149
and remember our grandparents.

355
00:13:19,349 --> 00:13:22,519
It'd be nice to invite my friends from time to time.

356
00:13:23,189 --> 00:13:24,919
I just don't want this house...

357
00:13:26,189 --> 00:13:28,029
to disappear.

358
00:13:30,699 --> 00:13:32,998
- Okay. Let's grill meat. - Yes.

359
00:13:32,999 --> 00:13:34,728
- Let's cook ramyeon. - Yes.

360
00:13:34,729 --> 00:13:36,068
Who's going to cook ramyeon? Me?

361
00:13:36,069 --> 00:13:38,350
Yes. I think it's okay to eat my grandma's kimchi. Right?

362
00:13:38,709 --> 00:13:39,908
He's eyeing it.

363
00:13:39,909 --> 00:13:41,539
Let's just eat half a head of cabbage.

364
00:13:42,009 --> 00:13:44,309
(I doubt you can eat your grandma's kimchi.)

365
00:13:44,639 --> 00:13:47,109
(Isn't it better if we eat it for you?)

366
00:13:47,509 --> 00:13:49,348
In case she throws it out.

367
00:13:49,349 --> 00:13:50,678
- That's it, right? - Yes.

368
00:13:50,679 --> 00:13:53,189
- I wouldn't. - I hate wasting food. That's all.

369
00:13:53,419 --> 00:13:55,958
Since I don't have anything to do while you guys cook,

370
00:13:55,959 --> 00:13:57,288
I'll sort out the pictures.

371
00:13:57,289 --> 00:13:58,688
- The ones he took down earlier? - Yes.

372
00:13:58,689 --> 00:14:00,288
- Wipe them. - Yes.

373
00:14:00,289 --> 00:14:01,528
- I'll help you too. - We should get...

374
00:14:01,529 --> 00:14:02,759
the charcoal and meat then.

375
00:14:03,199 --> 00:14:04,259
Me too?

376
00:14:04,899 --> 00:14:06,429
Then help me with the pictures.

377
00:14:07,269 --> 00:14:08,398
Which task is easier?

378
00:14:08,399 --> 00:14:10,199
Come on.

379
00:14:11,139 --> 00:14:13,238
All of my clothes are ruined.

380
00:14:13,239 --> 00:14:15,208
- Who told you to wear them? - Right.

381
00:14:15,209 --> 00:14:16,438
- Who wears clothes like them there? - Come on.

382
00:14:16,439 --> 00:14:19,479
Who wears that kind of outfit to the countryside?

383
00:14:19,609 --> 00:14:22,519
Gosh, this is crazy. People see me as a fashionista.

384
00:14:23,019 --> 00:14:25,148
By the way, what's that thing on your head?

385
00:14:25,149 --> 00:14:27,149
- Isn't that a mask? - The mask from earlier.

386
00:14:27,219 --> 00:14:28,779
I didn't want my hairline to look thin.

387
00:14:29,819 --> 00:14:31,719
- For real? - That's why?

388
00:14:32,789 --> 00:14:34,958
(Your hair is thinning.)

389
00:14:34,959 --> 00:14:36,659
(Thin)

390
00:14:37,159 --> 00:14:39,128
- That's why I did that. - I had no idea.

391
00:14:39,129 --> 00:14:40,868
- You thought it was for style? - Yes.

392
00:14:40,869 --> 00:14:43,298
You must have hurt Moorincess' feelings.

393
00:14:43,299 --> 00:14:44,369
(Feeling bitter)

394
00:14:44,869 --> 00:14:46,469
I'll get the charcoal ready then.

395
00:14:47,469 --> 00:14:49,609
- We don't have the brazier here. - Okay.

396
00:14:50,139 --> 00:14:52,348
- Fill the cauldron with charcoal. - Yes.

397
00:14:52,349 --> 00:14:53,578
- You can do that. - This should work.

398
00:14:53,579 --> 00:14:55,549
- We can put the grill on top. - Yes.

399
00:14:56,849 --> 00:14:57,919
All right.

400
00:14:57,949 --> 00:15:00,848
(They bring out the frames, so they can wipe the frames.)

401
00:15:00,849 --> 00:15:02,689
We need to dust the frames.

402
00:15:03,059 --> 00:15:04,658
Na Rae, who are they?

403
00:15:04,659 --> 00:15:07,088
They're my cousins.

404
00:15:07,089 --> 00:15:08,998
- I'm the one on the bottom. - Okay.

405
00:15:08,999 --> 00:15:10,899
- This is you? Right here? - Yes.

406
00:15:11,299 --> 00:15:12,369
Yes.

407
00:15:15,029 --> 00:15:16,769
This is you? How old were you then?

408
00:15:18,739 --> 00:15:20,259
You look like you started school late.

409
00:15:20,639 --> 00:15:21,668
You were a kid then?

410
00:15:21,669 --> 00:15:23,108
- She looks determined. - Yes.

411
00:15:23,109 --> 00:15:24,808
I ruled my neighborhood back then.

412
00:15:24,809 --> 00:15:25,908
It's my graduation photo from kindergarten.

413
00:15:25,909 --> 00:15:27,449
- From kindergarten? - Yes.

414
00:15:27,509 --> 00:15:29,069
Not from the Lifelong Education Center?

415
00:15:29,479 --> 00:15:31,148
- Lifelong Education? - Why do I look so old?

416
00:15:31,149 --> 00:15:32,749
- Are you sure? - Yes.

417
00:15:35,189 --> 00:15:36,259
Gosh, it's so dusty.

418
00:15:37,019 --> 00:15:38,729
- It was so dusty. - All right.

419
00:15:39,029 --> 00:15:40,829
- All right. - He wiped everything.

420
00:15:41,559 --> 00:15:44,429
- Gosh, it was so dusty. - It easily collects dust.

421
00:15:45,369 --> 00:15:47,898
Once you finish putting up the new wallpaper,

422
00:15:47,899 --> 00:15:48,968
they will look great...

423
00:15:48,969 --> 00:15:49,999
- on the wall. - Right.

424
00:15:50,399 --> 00:15:53,068
I'll go and bring the meat and everything in the fridge.

425
00:15:53,069 --> 00:15:55,109
- Okay. - Just start the fire.

426
00:15:55,169 --> 00:15:57,339
(Hyun Moo goes in to grab the ingredients.)

427
00:15:58,279 --> 00:15:59,509
- Kian84. - Yes?

428
00:15:59,779 --> 00:16:01,209
You worked a lot today.

429
00:16:01,649 --> 00:16:03,449
- Eat this? - What's that?

430
00:16:05,019 --> 00:16:06,219
I'm so exhausted.

431
00:16:06,419 --> 00:16:08,018
We worked a lot today.

432
00:16:08,019 --> 00:16:09,919
Since we're tired, let's take one each.

433
00:16:10,159 --> 00:16:11,439
At this rate, we might pass out.

434
00:16:11,759 --> 00:16:13,959
(Let's recover from fatigue before we pass out.)

435
00:16:14,159 --> 00:16:16,399
Taking this supplement will make a huge difference.

436
00:16:16,459 --> 00:16:17,959
- We should take one. - Yes.

437
00:16:19,229 --> 00:16:21,699
- You should. - We should.

438
00:16:22,699 --> 00:16:24,138
(Swallowing)

439
00:16:24,139 --> 00:16:25,268
This is nice.

440
00:16:25,269 --> 00:16:26,608
We must start taking supplements.

441
00:16:26,609 --> 00:16:28,609
We're not those young people from eight years ago.

442
00:16:29,439 --> 00:16:30,778
(Let's get started now that my energy is up.)

443
00:16:30,779 --> 00:16:32,879
- I'll set it up here. - Sure.

444
00:16:35,049 --> 00:16:36,819
My gosh.

445
00:16:37,319 --> 00:16:39,389
(Adding the charcoal to the cauldron)

446
00:16:39,819 --> 00:16:41,288
- Do you want the meat now? - What?

447
00:16:41,289 --> 00:16:42,919
- Do you want the meat? - Yes.

448
00:16:43,319 --> 00:16:45,039
I'll bring it over once you start the fire.

449
00:16:45,359 --> 00:16:46,958
- Hyun Moo. - Yes.

450
00:16:46,959 --> 00:16:48,389
I brought a photo album.

451
00:16:48,799 --> 00:16:49,998
Put the photos in it then.

452
00:16:49,999 --> 00:16:52,099
- Sure. - It's in my bag.

453
00:16:52,399 --> 00:16:53,998
You brought an empty photo album?

454
00:16:53,999 --> 00:16:55,168
He bought one.

455
00:16:55,169 --> 00:16:56,898
Our photo album was too old.

456
00:16:56,899 --> 00:16:59,768
She told me that she had a lot of pictures.

457
00:16:59,769 --> 00:17:01,909
I bought one, so we could organize them.

458
00:17:04,039 --> 00:17:06,609
(He comes out with the album and meat.)

459
00:17:07,609 --> 00:17:08,708
Here.

460
00:17:08,709 --> 00:17:09,879
(Passing the meat to Na Rae)

461
00:17:10,449 --> 00:17:12,489
(Blazing up)

462
00:17:14,389 --> 00:17:15,888
It looks like their roles have switched.

463
00:17:15,889 --> 00:17:16,918
(Starting the fire without hesitation)

464
00:17:16,919 --> 00:17:19,758
- How sharp. - It felt natural for us, though.

465
00:17:19,759 --> 00:17:21,789
(Sitting down delicately)

466
00:17:23,799 --> 00:17:25,829
Everyone can just do what they're good at.

467
00:17:28,169 --> 00:17:30,445
- Gosh, look at the dust. - Goodness, it's nice and breezy.

468
00:17:30,469 --> 00:17:32,708
- The breeze feels refreshing. - I can breathe again.

469
00:17:32,709 --> 00:17:34,509
Seriously. Thank you for your hard work.

470
00:17:34,839 --> 00:17:36,479
I'll arrange them myself.

471
00:17:36,779 --> 00:17:37,939
- Okay. - I mean, the photos.

472
00:17:39,049 --> 00:17:41,879
(Aligning them neatly)

473
00:17:43,449 --> 00:17:44,519
My gosh.

474
00:17:45,849 --> 00:17:46,918
When was that taken?

475
00:17:46,919 --> 00:17:48,199
(This looks different from now.)

476
00:17:48,519 --> 00:17:50,288
Hey, the door used to be here.

477
00:17:50,289 --> 00:17:52,728
- Yes. - Is that the same house?

478
00:17:52,729 --> 00:17:54,688
Yes. It's exactly the same house.

479
00:17:54,689 --> 00:17:55,999
Only the door was moved.

480
00:17:56,129 --> 00:17:57,398
Is it okay that I'm arranging...

481
00:17:57,399 --> 00:17:59,099
- your grandparents' photos? - Yes.

482
00:17:59,169 --> 00:18:01,368
I'm trying to tell a story with the photos.

483
00:18:01,369 --> 00:18:03,898
I think it will be too much on me.

484
00:18:03,899 --> 00:18:05,269
- Is that so? - Yes.

485
00:18:05,939 --> 00:18:07,179
They look young in the picture.

486
00:18:07,709 --> 00:18:09,078
They were young in this picture.

487
00:18:09,079 --> 00:18:10,278
You guys are a real family.

488
00:18:10,279 --> 00:18:11,479
- They were young. - Right.

489
00:18:11,639 --> 00:18:13,439
You're paying attention while arranging them.

490
00:18:15,349 --> 00:18:16,648
(Na Rae gets ready to grill meat.)

491
00:18:16,649 --> 00:18:18,618
Na Rae, you look like the owner of a famous restaurant.

492
00:18:18,619 --> 00:18:19,819
- Yes. - Right.

493
00:18:20,319 --> 00:18:22,088
You arranged the pork belly like how it looked in the photos.

494
00:18:22,089 --> 00:18:24,888
Gosh, you bet. I have my own story to tell with the pork belly.

495
00:18:24,889 --> 00:18:26,788
(Using the torch on the neatly arranged pork belly)

496
00:18:26,789 --> 00:18:28,359
I should let the oil drip.

497
00:18:29,759 --> 00:18:30,898
I needed the oil to drip...

498
00:18:30,899 --> 00:18:31,998
- to start the fire. - That's it.

499
00:18:31,999 --> 00:18:34,099
- It was so good. - Yes.

500
00:18:39,739 --> 00:18:41,309
My gosh. Darn it.

501
00:18:42,209 --> 00:18:43,608
My gosh. Darn it.

502
00:18:43,609 --> 00:18:45,238
(Falling out)

503
00:18:45,239 --> 00:18:46,448
- Oh, no. - My gosh.

504
00:18:46,449 --> 00:18:47,609
Darn it.

505
00:18:47,849 --> 00:18:49,349
(Glancing over)

506
00:18:49,979 --> 00:18:51,049
Darn it.

507
00:18:51,849 --> 00:18:53,019
Darn it.

508
00:18:55,792 --> 00:18:57,872
(She's grilling the meat for the hard-working guys.)

509
00:18:59,571 --> 00:19:01,670
- My gosh. Darn it. - Oh, no.

510
00:19:01,671 --> 00:19:03,411
- You can pick it up quickly. - Yes.

511
00:19:05,611 --> 00:19:07,110
- It's okay. Put it back. - Yes. Let's do that.

512
00:19:07,111 --> 00:19:08,310
- No. - No, don't throw it out.

513
00:19:08,311 --> 00:19:09,850
- You might die from it. - I'll eat that.

514
00:19:09,851 --> 00:19:11,357
- My gosh. - Your appendix will burst.

515
00:19:11,381 --> 00:19:13,990
- Why throw it out? Put it back. - I won't eat it!

516
00:19:13,991 --> 00:19:15,790
- Put it back on the grill! - No! I won't eat that.

517
00:19:15,791 --> 00:19:17,190
- I'll never eat it. - I'll eat that.

518
00:19:17,191 --> 00:19:18,721
- No, don't do it. - Let me just...

519
00:19:18,991 --> 00:19:20,831
(Anxious)

520
00:19:22,091 --> 00:19:24,131
(Calm down, guys.)

521
00:19:24,461 --> 00:19:26,230
- Put it back on the grill! - No! I won't eat that.

522
00:19:26,231 --> 00:19:27,670
- I'll never eat it. - I'll eat that.

523
00:19:27,671 --> 00:19:28,940
- No, don't do it. - Let me just...

524
00:19:28,941 --> 00:19:30,240
- I'll eat that. - I won't eat it.

525
00:19:30,241 --> 00:19:31,800
- I'll eat that. - Your appendix will burst.

526
00:19:31,801 --> 00:19:33,611
- It will be my appendix bursting. - Shut it.

527
00:19:33,811 --> 00:19:36,410
- Let me wash it with water. - I won't eat it.

528
00:19:36,411 --> 00:19:37,581
- My gosh. - I'll eat that.

529
00:19:38,511 --> 00:19:39,980
(Bickering over meat)

530
00:19:39,981 --> 00:19:41,510
- Just rinse it with water. - Seriously.

531
00:19:41,511 --> 00:19:42,857
- I don't eat dirty food. - I won't eat it.

532
00:19:42,881 --> 00:19:45,080
- I said I'd eat it. - It's got soil on it.

533
00:19:45,081 --> 00:19:46,690
And it's muddy here. No, don't do it.

534
00:19:46,691 --> 00:19:48,051
- It's okay. - No, don't do it.

535
00:19:48,751 --> 00:19:50,820
This is why your immune system is weak.

536
00:19:50,821 --> 00:19:51,921
What are you talking about?

537
00:19:52,361 --> 00:19:54,307
Your immune system gets weak from eating food like that.

538
00:19:54,331 --> 00:19:55,730
- Just rinse it with water. - Rinse it with water.

539
00:19:55,731 --> 00:19:57,500
- No. - He was dead set against it.

540
00:19:57,501 --> 00:19:58,800
- Never. - Just rinse it with water.

541
00:19:58,801 --> 00:19:59,801
I hate dirty things.

542
00:19:59,802 --> 00:20:02,770
And the soil is full of germs.

543
00:20:02,771 --> 00:20:04,370
- That's my point. - The fire would kill them all.

544
00:20:04,371 --> 00:20:06,070
No. It doesn't work like that.

545
00:20:06,071 --> 00:20:07,641
You could die from it.

546
00:20:08,111 --> 00:20:10,111
- Our lives are too sheltered. - Right.

547
00:20:10,381 --> 00:20:11,610
- You could die from it. - Just pretend that it's clean...

548
00:20:11,611 --> 00:20:12,680
and just eat it.

549
00:20:12,681 --> 00:20:14,150
- Yes. - Yes. Do that.

550
00:20:14,151 --> 00:20:15,550
- Yes. - You did that?

551
00:20:15,551 --> 00:20:17,480
No, I just washed it.

552
00:20:17,481 --> 00:20:18,991
It was on the ground for a second.

553
00:20:19,921 --> 00:20:22,420
Then the meat will just burn without getting cooked.

554
00:20:22,421 --> 00:20:24,290
No, I'm watching it constantly.

555
00:20:24,291 --> 00:20:25,291
(Acting as if nothing happened)

556
00:20:25,292 --> 00:20:26,331
(Glancing back)

557
00:20:27,191 --> 00:20:28,930
- Look. It looks so milky-white. - Come on.

558
00:20:28,931 --> 00:20:30,861
- Did I eat that? - It looks so clean.

559
00:20:35,131 --> 00:20:36,570
(The meat is getting grilled nicely.)

560
00:20:36,571 --> 00:20:38,201
I'm doing an amazing job with this.

561
00:20:38,971 --> 00:20:41,252
- Na Rae, put it back. It's a waste. - Yes, I'll do that.

562
00:20:41,411 --> 00:20:42,411
Put what back? Don't.

563
00:20:42,412 --> 00:20:44,317
- I'll eat the one that fell. - No. Don't eat it.

564
00:20:44,341 --> 00:20:46,010
- You shouldn't eat dirty food. - You shouldn't.

565
00:20:46,011 --> 00:20:47,487
Why eat something that fell into the muddy water?

566
00:20:47,511 --> 00:20:49,420
We all start in the soil and return to it anyway.

567
00:20:49,421 --> 00:20:51,781
- What's so bad about... - You'll have a quick return then.

568
00:20:52,721 --> 00:20:54,390
But pigs grow up around the soil.

569
00:20:54,391 --> 00:20:55,420
- Yes. - Of course.

570
00:20:55,421 --> 00:20:57,101
Why don't you just grill meat on the dirt?

571
00:20:57,261 --> 00:20:58,821
Just place it on the dirt and torch it.

572
00:20:59,061 --> 00:21:00,330
- Wipe off the dirt afterward. - Seriously.

573
00:21:00,331 --> 00:21:01,661
That way, I won't drop it.

574
00:21:02,431 --> 00:21:05,001
(The rinsed meat is getting cooked nicely.)

575
00:21:07,931 --> 00:21:09,451
- Gosh, she grilled it well. - Kian84.

576
00:21:09,801 --> 00:21:11,571
(Smacking his lips)

577
00:21:11,941 --> 00:21:12,941
Kian84.

578
00:21:13,541 --> 00:21:15,141
- I'm nearly done grilling meat. - Okay.

579
00:21:15,371 --> 00:21:17,911
- Once I wrap this up, - Okay.

580
00:21:18,111 --> 00:21:19,781
I'll bring kimchi.

581
00:21:20,111 --> 00:21:22,250
Would it really be okay to eat your grandma's kimchi?

582
00:21:22,251 --> 00:21:23,251
It's okay.

583
00:21:23,921 --> 00:21:26,020
I felt like Grandma would be happy to see us eat it.

584
00:21:26,021 --> 00:21:27,021
That's right.

585
00:21:27,391 --> 00:21:28,520
(Taking out her grandma's kimchi...)

586
00:21:28,521 --> 00:21:29,660
(makes her feel conflicted)

587
00:21:29,661 --> 00:21:31,261
I know that Grandma's kimchi is here.

588
00:21:32,261 --> 00:21:34,631
(Let's see.)

589
00:21:34,891 --> 00:21:36,601
It's here. It's aged kimchi.

590
00:21:37,801 --> 00:21:39,801
It was made in 2022.

591
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:43,501
(Aged kimchi her grandma made in 2022)

592
00:21:43,502 --> 00:21:45,140
- Year of 2022? - Year of 2022.

593
00:21:45,141 --> 00:21:46,610
- It's three years old. - Yes.

594
00:21:46,611 --> 00:21:48,571
It had an address on the top.

595
00:21:48,941 --> 00:21:52,380
She was going to send it to my place.

596
00:21:52,381 --> 00:21:53,951
Was the address yours?

597
00:21:54,051 --> 00:21:56,681
She couldn't send it, so it was right there.

598
00:21:58,621 --> 00:22:00,720
(She opens the aged kimchi.)

599
00:22:00,721 --> 00:22:01,991
This won't do.

600
00:22:03,521 --> 00:22:04,560
Since it was made in 2022...

601
00:22:04,561 --> 00:22:06,290
- You can eat it. - You can eat it.

602
00:22:06,291 --> 00:22:07,330
That's right.

603
00:22:07,331 --> 00:22:09,360
Since it's only yeast,

604
00:22:09,361 --> 00:22:10,861
I can rinse it and eat it.

605
00:22:13,901 --> 00:22:15,940
You can rinse it and make kimchi jjigae with it.

606
00:22:15,941 --> 00:22:16,970
I didn't know that you could save it.

607
00:22:16,971 --> 00:22:17,971
- You can save it. - I see.

608
00:22:17,972 --> 00:22:19,140
Isn't the bottom part like that?

609
00:22:19,141 --> 00:22:21,021
- No, not at all. - The bottom part looks fine.

610
00:22:24,911 --> 00:22:26,380
- Hyun Moo. - Yes?

611
00:22:26,381 --> 00:22:27,550
(Hyun Moo)

612
00:22:27,551 --> 00:22:29,250
- I don't think... - Yes?

613
00:22:29,251 --> 00:22:31,081
- we can eat this kimchi. - Why not?

614
00:22:31,751 --> 00:22:33,121
- Well... - Did it go bad?

615
00:22:33,251 --> 00:22:35,251
We could rinse it and eat it, but it'd be a bit...

616
00:22:35,491 --> 00:22:37,461
You should be the judge. We don't need to eat it.

617
00:22:38,221 --> 00:22:40,061
Let's make ramyeon. We don't need to eat it.

618
00:22:40,531 --> 00:22:42,051
I wanted to treat them to a nice meal.

619
00:22:42,191 --> 00:22:43,600
But the white stuff isn't mold.

620
00:22:43,601 --> 00:22:46,430
You can remove it, rinse the kimchi, and eat it.

621
00:22:46,431 --> 00:22:49,070
But I couldn't include that kimchi in the meal...

622
00:22:49,071 --> 00:22:50,501
for Hyun Moo and Kian84.

623
00:22:50,871 --> 00:22:53,241
I felt like my grandma would be upset.

624
00:22:53,571 --> 00:22:55,980
Both my grandma and grandpa told me...

625
00:22:55,981 --> 00:22:58,541
that I needed to treat my guests well.

626
00:22:59,281 --> 00:23:01,401
They would've scolded me if I had served that kimchi.

627
00:23:02,881 --> 00:23:05,080
I'll give you a nice meal.

628
00:23:05,081 --> 00:23:07,441
- Okay. I'll look forward to it. - It'll be a proper feast.

629
00:23:08,691 --> 00:23:10,291
- Here. - My goodness.

630
00:23:10,461 --> 00:23:12,590
These things were in the house.

631
00:23:12,591 --> 00:23:13,591
- Okay. - What?

632
00:23:13,592 --> 00:23:15,190
- Disposable chopsticks. - Disposable chopsticks? Nice.

633
00:23:15,191 --> 00:23:16,500
(Na Rae brings disposable items and a pot.)

634
00:23:16,501 --> 00:23:18,231
Gosh, it's an enamel pot.

635
00:23:18,701 --> 00:23:19,870
- You haven't seen one, right? - Yes.

636
00:23:19,871 --> 00:23:21,070
It's really old.

637
00:23:21,071 --> 00:23:22,800
- Grandma used it well. - It looks nice.

638
00:23:22,801 --> 00:23:24,881
- They don't throw away such items. - It looks cute.

639
00:23:25,341 --> 00:23:27,771
- I think Grandma will scold me... - Why?

640
00:23:28,141 --> 00:23:29,610
For mistreating my guests.

641
00:23:29,611 --> 00:23:31,211
Don't be ridiculous.

642
00:23:31,411 --> 00:23:33,531
Had she been alive, she would've scolded me so badly.

643
00:23:33,981 --> 00:23:35,850
This is enough. We have meat and ramyeon.

644
00:23:35,851 --> 00:23:36,920
This is enough. We just need ramyeon.

645
00:23:36,921 --> 00:23:38,881
- Still, I feel bad. - It's okay.

646
00:23:40,251 --> 00:23:41,491
I'll cook ramyeon.

647
00:23:42,051 --> 00:23:43,931
I have confidence in my ramyeon-cooking skills.

648
00:23:44,261 --> 00:23:46,160
- Adjust the volume of water. - I'll do that.

649
00:23:46,161 --> 00:23:47,231
The thing is,

650
00:23:47,491 --> 00:23:50,261
I had something delivered by someone I know.

651
00:23:50,501 --> 00:23:51,561
Like what?

652
00:23:51,631 --> 00:23:54,031
- Do you have acquaintances here? - Of course.

653
00:23:55,571 --> 00:23:56,641
It's makgeolli!

654
00:23:57,471 --> 00:23:58,500
(Heave-ho)

655
00:23:58,501 --> 00:24:00,171
- Nice. - Is it makgeolli?

656
00:24:00,741 --> 00:24:01,811
It's makgeolli.

657
00:24:02,511 --> 00:24:03,740
Take some bottles later.

658
00:24:03,741 --> 00:24:06,010
You can't order fewer than 20 bottles at a time...

659
00:24:06,011 --> 00:24:07,957
- if you want them delivered. - Really? I'll take some bottles.

660
00:24:07,981 --> 00:24:10,781
- Why did no one come to help? - My goodness.

661
00:24:10,981 --> 00:24:12,220
She would've felt uncomfortable.

662
00:24:12,221 --> 00:24:13,651
(What?)

663
00:24:13,991 --> 00:24:15,250
Most people pretend to get up...

664
00:24:15,251 --> 00:24:16,790
- even if they don't want to help. - I know.

665
00:24:16,791 --> 00:24:18,590
That would've been more unbecoming.

666
00:24:18,591 --> 00:24:21,330
Most people would've yelled from a distance, "Isn't it heavy?"

667
00:24:21,331 --> 00:24:23,800
They would've gone, "Oh, my!" They didn't do anything.

668
00:24:23,801 --> 00:24:25,200
INTPs are clear-cut.

669
00:24:25,201 --> 00:24:26,677
They don't pretend if they don't want to do something.

670
00:24:26,701 --> 00:24:27,930
They don't even go, "Oh, my!"

671
00:24:27,931 --> 00:24:29,271
I'm not surprised.

672
00:24:30,071 --> 00:24:31,270
- What kind of makgeolli is it? - Is it makgeolli?

673
00:24:31,271 --> 00:24:32,670
- Yes. - Where is it from?

674
00:24:32,671 --> 00:24:34,870
It's unpasteurized makgeolli.

675
00:24:34,871 --> 00:24:36,140
(It's even unpasteurized makgeolli.)

676
00:24:36,141 --> 00:24:37,241
- It's... - Give me one.

677
00:24:37,481 --> 00:24:39,180
The lager in the makgeolli scene.

678
00:24:39,181 --> 00:24:41,010
- The lager in the makgeolli scene? - The taste is so clean.

679
00:24:41,011 --> 00:24:43,411
That's why unpasteurized makgeolli has a shorter shelf life.

680
00:24:43,651 --> 00:24:45,250
Good makgeolli has a short shelf life.

681
00:24:45,251 --> 00:24:46,251
Yes.

682
00:24:46,321 --> 00:24:48,090
Hey, Na Rae. Wait, what was his name?

683
00:24:48,091 --> 00:24:49,221
For goodness' sake.

684
00:24:49,391 --> 00:24:51,021
- Take it. - You got confused.

685
00:24:51,321 --> 00:24:53,461
- All done. The meat is all done. - It's all done.

686
00:24:54,631 --> 00:24:55,900
The meat looks tasty.

687
00:24:55,901 --> 00:24:57,030
- It's all done. - You get that at a barbecue place.

688
00:24:57,031 --> 00:24:58,901
- It nearly looks like bacon. - Yes.

689
00:24:59,731 --> 00:25:01,870
Hey, this meal is too simple. Seriously.

690
00:25:01,871 --> 00:25:03,670
- It isn't simple at all. - I'm so embarrassed.

691
00:25:03,671 --> 00:25:05,400
- Seriously. - Take this.

692
00:25:05,401 --> 00:25:06,540
This is great.

693
00:25:06,541 --> 00:25:08,740
- Take this. - Gosh, thank you.

694
00:25:08,741 --> 00:25:10,210
- Thank you for your hard work. - All right.

695
00:25:10,211 --> 00:25:11,280
That's the spirit, Hyun Moo.

696
00:25:11,281 --> 00:25:13,451
- Cheers. - Thank you for your hard work.

697
00:25:13,981 --> 00:25:15,411
For Grandpa and Grandma.

698
00:25:15,551 --> 00:25:16,651
Grandma.

699
00:25:17,881 --> 00:25:20,521
You should've said something before downing it.

700
00:25:21,021 --> 00:25:23,160
- Grandma. For goodness' sake. - Oh, my.

701
00:25:23,161 --> 00:25:25,020
(I was too thirsty.)

702
00:25:25,021 --> 00:25:26,391
It's okay.

703
00:25:27,691 --> 00:25:28,830
It's light.

704
00:25:28,831 --> 00:25:30,400
That was why I called it lager. The taste is clean.

705
00:25:30,401 --> 00:25:31,500
The taste is clean.

706
00:25:31,501 --> 00:25:32,671
- It isn't rich. - No.

707
00:25:33,001 --> 00:25:35,081
(He takes a bite of the heavily grilled pork belly.)

708
00:25:35,501 --> 00:25:36,501
(Impressed)

709
00:25:38,801 --> 00:25:39,811
(Impressed)

710
00:25:40,941 --> 00:25:42,141
Look at the inside.

711
00:25:42,711 --> 00:25:43,780
It's insane.

712
00:25:43,781 --> 00:25:45,040
It's so juicy.

713
00:25:45,041 --> 00:25:47,181
- Nice. - Why is unseasoned meat so tasty?

714
00:25:47,281 --> 00:25:48,350
Did you season it with salt?

715
00:25:48,351 --> 00:25:49,421
- No. - No.

716
00:25:49,951 --> 00:25:51,320
Kian84, it's so tasty.

717
00:25:51,321 --> 00:25:52,851
- He grilled it well. - It's so tasty.

718
00:25:53,521 --> 00:25:54,820
It was tasty.

719
00:25:54,821 --> 00:25:56,821
- It was really tasty. - Yes.

720
00:25:57,221 --> 00:25:58,691
That sentimentality is nice.

721
00:25:59,791 --> 00:26:01,231
Hyun Moo, hurry up and cook ramyeon.

722
00:26:01,591 --> 00:26:02,631
Wait.

723
00:26:04,731 --> 00:26:06,931
- We cleaned everything, right? - Yes.

724
00:26:07,201 --> 00:26:09,107
Camellia flowers will grow over there in winter.

725
00:26:09,131 --> 00:26:11,641
It'll be nice to look at the camellia flowers in winter.

726
00:26:11,841 --> 00:26:13,011
Are we coming back in winter?

727
00:26:13,711 --> 00:26:15,070
I'm a bit scared to come back here.

728
00:26:15,071 --> 00:26:17,481
Don't be scared. Come here to rest then.

729
00:26:17,611 --> 00:26:18,611
- To rest? - Don't do anything.

730
00:26:18,612 --> 00:26:20,811
- I work a lot whenever I see you. - No way.

731
00:26:21,211 --> 00:26:23,020
Even if you come here to rest, there'll be some work to do.

732
00:26:23,021 --> 00:26:24,081
- I know. - No way.

733
00:26:24,681 --> 00:26:26,791
We need to work on the outer walls.

734
00:26:27,921 --> 00:26:29,420
- He needs to draw murals. - Yes.

735
00:26:29,421 --> 00:26:30,761
Kian84 is good at it.

736
00:26:31,391 --> 00:26:32,391
Thank you.

737
00:26:32,392 --> 00:26:33,552
(You know how I feel, right?)

738
00:26:33,861 --> 00:26:35,031
For you guys...

739
00:26:35,431 --> 00:26:37,400
- I hope this never happens. - Okay.

740
00:26:37,401 --> 00:26:38,800
But I'm willing to give you a kidney.

741
00:26:38,801 --> 00:26:40,521
- Really? - Why would you give me a kidney?

742
00:26:40,671 --> 00:26:42,001
I'll keep that in mind.

743
00:26:42,941 --> 00:26:45,000
To be honest, I could just hire a cleaning company.

744
00:26:45,001 --> 00:26:46,011
Right.

745
00:26:47,211 --> 00:26:50,211
But they wouldn't have wiped the photos.

746
00:26:50,381 --> 00:26:52,510
While professional cleaners are good,

747
00:26:52,511 --> 00:26:54,880
- it's meaningful for us to do it. - It's meaningful.

748
00:26:54,881 --> 00:26:56,681
That's right. We have memories of this place.

749
00:26:57,251 --> 00:26:58,920
Thank you for taking down the photos.

750
00:26:58,921 --> 00:27:00,667
I wanted to take them down, but couldn't bear to do it.

751
00:27:00,691 --> 00:27:02,327
- It would've been too much for you. - Yes.

752
00:27:02,351 --> 00:27:04,260
You wouldn't have been able to do it.

753
00:27:04,261 --> 00:27:05,261
Of course not.

754
00:27:05,861 --> 00:27:07,030
I was so thankful.

755
00:27:07,031 --> 00:27:09,071
(She's thankful for the people who understand her.)

756
00:27:09,331 --> 00:27:11,001
- I'm sorry to ruin the moment, - Okay.

757
00:27:11,031 --> 00:27:12,930
- but I'm so good at making ramyeon. - This is great.

758
00:27:12,931 --> 00:27:14,170
- It's insane. - Sprinkle some pepper.

759
00:27:14,171 --> 00:27:15,300
- Shall I add some pepper? - It'll be nice.

760
00:27:15,301 --> 00:27:16,870
- What has gotten into you? - Ramyeon tastes good with pepper.

761
00:27:16,871 --> 00:27:18,241
- Really? - Yes.

762
00:27:18,701 --> 00:27:20,170
(Sprinkling)

763
00:27:20,171 --> 00:27:21,441
Aren't you sprinkling too much?

764
00:27:23,211 --> 00:27:24,280
- Hyun Moo! - That's too much.

765
00:27:24,281 --> 00:27:25,921
It's okay because there's a lot of water.

766
00:27:25,981 --> 00:27:27,280
It's like this only on the surface.

767
00:27:27,281 --> 00:27:28,580
- Seriously. - It became jjajang ramyeon.

768
00:27:28,581 --> 00:27:29,681
Don't be ridiculous.

769
00:27:30,321 --> 00:27:32,280
- Will you work as well? - Yes. Isn't it too short?

770
00:27:32,281 --> 00:27:33,291
Seriously.

771
00:27:34,051 --> 00:27:35,120
It's spicy for him too.

772
00:27:35,121 --> 00:27:36,560
See? You sprinkled too much.

773
00:27:36,561 --> 00:27:37,967
(The added amount makes even Pepper Brother sneeze.)

774
00:27:37,991 --> 00:27:39,630
- I made it a bit salty. - A bit salty.

775
00:27:39,631 --> 00:27:41,561
- We need salt after sweating. - That's right.

776
00:27:42,031 --> 00:27:44,401
- It looks tasty. - It's good to make it a bit salty.

777
00:27:44,861 --> 00:27:46,931
The texture is firm and the taste is peppery.

778
00:27:47,901 --> 00:27:49,331
(Slurping)

779
00:27:50,841 --> 00:27:52,100
(Impressed)

780
00:27:52,101 --> 00:27:53,310
Seriously.

781
00:27:53,311 --> 00:27:54,971
It's insanely tasty!

782
00:27:55,711 --> 00:27:57,311
You should admit it.

783
00:27:57,711 --> 00:28:00,011
I eyeballed the amount of water.

784
00:28:00,781 --> 00:28:02,450
I'm better than the ramyeon machines at the Han River.

785
00:28:02,451 --> 00:28:03,580
(His eyeball measurement beats the ramyeon machines.)

786
00:28:03,581 --> 00:28:05,581
Seriously, Hyun Moo made it well.

787
00:28:07,491 --> 00:28:08,951
How is it so tasty?

788
00:28:09,391 --> 00:28:11,111
You can't beat me when it comes to ramyeon.

789
00:28:11,191 --> 00:28:12,721
- It's insanely tasty. - It's tasty.

790
00:28:14,031 --> 00:28:15,990
Food you eat after labor is really tasty.

791
00:28:15,991 --> 00:28:19,001
(They enjoy ramyeon after taxing labor.)

792
00:28:19,801 --> 00:28:20,831
It's good.

793
00:28:21,531 --> 00:28:24,500
The air was cool after the rain.

794
00:28:24,501 --> 00:28:26,941
(They eat as they cool their sweat in the cool evening wind.)

795
00:28:26,971 --> 00:28:28,071
It's really tasty.

796
00:28:28,971 --> 00:28:30,081
Have a drink.

797
00:28:30,781 --> 00:28:32,340
- Have a drink. - Okay.

798
00:28:32,341 --> 00:28:33,821
- You worked hard. - You worked hard.

799
00:28:33,951 --> 00:28:35,951
I'll treat you to a nice meal.

800
00:28:37,351 --> 00:28:39,181
(Hold on.)

801
00:28:40,021 --> 00:28:41,920
I think my grandma will scold me for not giving you...

802
00:28:41,921 --> 00:28:43,391
better drinking snacks.

803
00:28:44,221 --> 00:28:45,960
(What is it?)

804
00:28:45,961 --> 00:28:47,960
I'm taking it out even though I cherish it a lot.

805
00:28:47,961 --> 00:28:49,391
- What? What is it? - What is it?

806
00:28:50,361 --> 00:28:51,530
It's the salt cured fish roe I made.

807
00:28:51,531 --> 00:28:52,730
(The salt cured fish roe Na Rae made)

808
00:28:52,731 --> 00:28:53,731
The salt cured fish roe.

809
00:28:53,871 --> 00:28:55,171
- Did you make it? - Yes.

810
00:28:56,301 --> 00:28:58,100
You need to wipe it twice a day.

811
00:28:58,101 --> 00:28:59,940
(She learned the method...)

812
00:28:59,941 --> 00:29:01,810
(from the salt cured fish roe master at Mount Jiri.)

813
00:29:01,811 --> 00:29:03,281
About four weeks.

814
00:29:03,641 --> 00:29:05,540
For three weeks,

815
00:29:05,541 --> 00:29:07,151
I wiped it twice a day with Munbaeju.

816
00:29:07,481 --> 00:29:08,680
Now, I keep it...

817
00:29:08,681 --> 00:29:11,421
in a vacuum-sealed bag.

818
00:29:11,851 --> 00:29:13,920
I flipped it over twice a day.

819
00:29:13,921 --> 00:29:14,990
That's a lot of work.

820
00:29:14,991 --> 00:29:16,921
- We don't have a knife. - There we go.

821
00:29:17,891 --> 00:29:20,191
Take a small piece and taste it.

822
00:29:20,361 --> 00:29:21,830
You're absolutely incredible.

823
00:29:21,831 --> 00:29:24,031
- How did you make that? - He sprinkled so much pepper.

824
00:29:24,131 --> 00:29:25,330
- He added more. - I should've known.

825
00:29:25,331 --> 00:29:26,370
I did it when they weren't looking.

826
00:29:26,371 --> 00:29:28,652
(Unaware, they focus on slicing the salt cured fish roe.)

827
00:29:28,871 --> 00:29:30,441
It's working.

828
00:29:31,401 --> 00:29:32,771
A bit at a time.

829
00:29:33,211 --> 00:29:34,610
- Try a small bit. - I'm really curious about it.

830
00:29:34,611 --> 00:29:36,171
- It's so precious. - It's so precious.

831
00:29:36,981 --> 00:29:38,010
I'll eat it as it is.

832
00:29:38,011 --> 00:29:39,771
Okay. Eat a small bit at a time. It's salty.

833
00:29:40,011 --> 00:29:41,511
- It's really tasty. - It's so tasty.

834
00:29:42,411 --> 00:29:44,481
- I love the chewy texture. - Yes.

835
00:29:45,051 --> 00:29:47,021
(Smacking his lips)

836
00:29:47,321 --> 00:29:48,751
(Impressed)

837
00:29:49,151 --> 00:29:50,591
I'm getting drunk.

838
00:29:51,121 --> 00:29:52,921
I'm getting drunk on the salt cured fish roe.

839
00:29:53,261 --> 00:29:54,530
Ramyeon with salt cured fish roe.

840
00:29:54,531 --> 00:29:56,007
(Let's try ramyeon with salt cured fish roe.)

841
00:29:56,031 --> 00:29:57,530
They don't even sell it in Naples.

842
00:29:57,531 --> 00:29:59,060
They have salt cured fish roe pasta, but not that.

843
00:29:59,061 --> 00:30:00,071
No.

844
00:30:02,731 --> 00:30:04,171
It's chewy.

845
00:30:04,841 --> 00:30:06,441
It's a bit funky.

846
00:30:07,811 --> 00:30:10,611
The oily taste hits you belatedly.

847
00:30:11,381 --> 00:30:13,511
It's such a luxury.

848
00:30:14,151 --> 00:30:15,981
When I made salt cured fish roe,

849
00:30:16,221 --> 00:30:17,880
I thought that I'd eat it on a special day.

850
00:30:17,881 --> 00:30:19,081
Today is a special day.

851
00:30:21,891 --> 00:30:22,891
(With people who are dear to her,)

852
00:30:22,892 --> 00:30:24,932
(she eats salt cured fish roe without reservation.)

853
00:30:26,021 --> 00:30:27,431
(Impressed)

854
00:30:27,731 --> 00:30:28,891
It's such a luxury.

855
00:30:30,131 --> 00:30:31,200
I'm getting drunk.

856
00:30:31,201 --> 00:30:33,201
(As they enjoy delicious food, the night deepens.)

857
00:30:33,271 --> 00:30:34,300
The thing is...

858
00:30:34,301 --> 00:30:36,741
(The thing is...)

859
00:30:37,671 --> 00:30:40,971
There's a Jindo dog my grandpa had.

860
00:30:41,241 --> 00:30:42,541
- It's named Bokdol. - Okay.

861
00:30:43,241 --> 00:30:45,411
- The dog at the animal hospital? - Yes.

862
00:30:46,711 --> 00:30:48,480
(Grandpa's dog was sent to the animal hospital...)

863
00:30:48,481 --> 00:30:49,950
(for a checkup.)

864
00:30:49,951 --> 00:30:51,427
- It's at the animal hospital. - Is it at the animal hospital?

865
00:30:51,451 --> 00:30:52,781
- It's named Bokdol. - Okay.

866
00:30:55,351 --> 00:30:58,220
My grandpa was the one who came up with the name, Bokdol.

867
00:30:58,221 --> 00:31:00,491
In a sense, that dog is your grandpa's child.

868
00:31:00,561 --> 00:31:03,131
The breed is called hogu.

869
00:31:03,431 --> 00:31:04,531
- Hogu? - Hogu?

870
00:31:04,631 --> 00:31:07,431
Baekgu, hwanggu, and heukgu are the types of Jindo dogs.

871
00:31:07,501 --> 00:31:09,131
- Like the word for "A pushover?" - Yes.

872
00:31:09,531 --> 00:31:10,901
No. The meaning is different.

873
00:31:11,241 --> 00:31:12,540
Hyun Moo is a pushover.

874
00:31:12,541 --> 00:31:14,087
I'm a pushover. Both of us are pushovers.

875
00:31:14,111 --> 00:31:17,180
It's a Jindo dog with tiger patterns.

876
00:31:17,181 --> 00:31:19,180
- "Ho" means "Tiger." - I know this type of Jindo dogs.

877
00:31:19,181 --> 00:31:20,910
Grandpa had it for two and a half years.

878
00:31:20,911 --> 00:31:23,881
For dogs, two and a half years is a long period of time.

879
00:31:24,421 --> 00:31:27,091
For about six months after Grandpa passed away,

880
00:31:27,791 --> 00:31:29,567
it didn't eat dog food when Uncle tried to feed it.

881
00:31:29,591 --> 00:31:31,291
- Jindo dogs are loyal. - Yes.

882
00:31:33,091 --> 00:31:36,431
The thing is, I... I bet I told you this once.

883
00:31:37,161 --> 00:31:40,001
To be honest, ever since Grandpa passed away...

884
00:31:40,601 --> 00:31:43,201
It has been over two years, right?

885
00:31:43,701 --> 00:31:45,101
I thought about the dog constantly.

886
00:31:46,541 --> 00:31:49,241
I gave it some thought earlier.

887
00:31:49,341 --> 00:31:51,757
While I shouldn't tell people what they should or shouldn't do...

888
00:31:51,781 --> 00:31:54,410
- It's okay. But I... - Why don't you take the dog?

889
00:31:54,411 --> 00:31:55,511
Really?

890
00:31:56,651 --> 00:31:58,021
Will I be a good owner?

891
00:31:59,321 --> 00:32:00,691
As for that...

892
00:32:01,491 --> 00:32:05,421
I think that dog will bring you good fortune.

893
00:32:05,621 --> 00:32:08,331
- Its name is Bokdol. - I believe in such things.

894
00:32:08,591 --> 00:32:09,731
Also, you could say...

895
00:32:10,861 --> 00:32:12,931
that your grandpa left you...

896
00:32:13,731 --> 00:32:16,601
- He left me the dog. - Yes. He left you the dog.

897
00:32:19,201 --> 00:32:21,161
- Take the dog in. I'll help you. - That's right.

898
00:32:21,541 --> 00:32:22,741
(Really?)

899
00:32:23,211 --> 00:32:25,411
I'll send you some dog food. Feed the dog.

900
00:32:27,181 --> 00:32:29,251
Take it home for now.

901
00:32:29,611 --> 00:32:33,091
If things get too tough, I'll recommend a training center.

902
00:32:33,821 --> 00:32:35,020
I'll take the dog tomorrow.

903
00:32:35,021 --> 00:32:36,090
- Tomorrow? - That's right.

904
00:32:36,091 --> 00:32:38,520
The dog is currently in Itaewon.

905
00:32:38,521 --> 00:32:40,361
- At your place? - Bokdol is at my place. Yes.

906
00:32:40,761 --> 00:32:42,190
I'm learning new things with Hyun Moo's help.

907
00:32:42,191 --> 00:32:44,530
We've been messaging each other a lot because of that.

908
00:32:44,531 --> 00:32:46,971
- That's right. - You should let the dog adjust.

909
00:32:47,131 --> 00:32:49,171
Bokdol is living well in Seoul.

910
00:32:50,401 --> 00:32:52,771
- Seriously, give it a go. - What?

911
00:32:54,771 --> 00:32:56,141
For goodness' sake.

912
00:32:57,041 --> 00:32:58,411
Why is it raining so suddenly?

913
00:32:58,541 --> 00:32:59,780
It's nice and cool.

914
00:32:59,781 --> 00:33:01,050
It began raining after we finished eating ramyeon.

915
00:33:01,051 --> 00:33:02,111
I can't believe it.

916
00:33:02,711 --> 00:33:04,321
- Gosh, this is... - For goodness' sake.

917
00:33:04,551 --> 00:33:06,321
It's nice. Let's just eat.

918
00:33:06,491 --> 00:33:08,221
- I'll lose hair again. - Really?

919
00:33:08,321 --> 00:33:10,591
Why is it raining so suddenly?

920
00:33:11,391 --> 00:33:14,031
- Heavy rain began so suddenly. - I think it'll stop raining soon.

921
00:33:14,261 --> 00:33:17,430
I think your grandparents watched over us today.

922
00:33:17,431 --> 00:33:20,430
Had it been hot like it said on the weather forecast,

923
00:33:20,431 --> 00:33:22,101
I would've passed out.

924
00:33:22,271 --> 00:33:23,770
I could keep going thanks to the cool wind.

925
00:33:23,771 --> 00:33:25,341
That's right. We would've passed out.

926
00:33:27,971 --> 00:33:30,481
- Yes. Like that, it rained. - It wasn't hot.

927
00:33:30,881 --> 00:33:32,961
- It was sunny when we grilled meat. - That's right.

928
00:33:34,681 --> 00:33:36,051
Since it's raining,

929
00:33:36,921 --> 00:33:38,401
I don't know if I can give it to you.

930
00:33:39,381 --> 00:33:40,491
What?

931
00:33:41,621 --> 00:33:42,751
You know,

932
00:33:43,691 --> 00:33:45,260
the weather worsened when I was about to give a present.

933
00:33:45,261 --> 00:33:46,491
(A present?)

934
00:33:47,661 --> 00:33:48,731
What is it?

935
00:33:49,598 --> 00:33:50,698
You know,

936
00:33:51,698 --> 00:33:53,197
the weather worsened when I was about to give a present.

937
00:33:53,198 --> 00:33:55,518
(The weather worsened when I was about to give a present.)

938
00:33:56,198 --> 00:33:58,767
- It's raining so much. - My present...

939
00:33:58,768 --> 00:34:00,038
shouldn't get rained on.

940
00:34:00,338 --> 00:34:02,007
Shouldn't it get rained on?

941
00:34:02,008 --> 00:34:03,438
Then show it to us quickly.

942
00:34:04,278 --> 00:34:06,608
Hold on. Let me bring an umbrella.

943
00:34:08,348 --> 00:34:10,577
It has been so long since I drank in the rain.

944
00:34:10,578 --> 00:34:11,878
(Screaming)

945
00:34:12,048 --> 00:34:13,887
- Are you okay? - My hip joints are ruined.

946
00:34:13,888 --> 00:34:15,528
- Oh, no. - My hip joints were destroyed.

947
00:34:16,558 --> 00:34:18,358
The rain feels so cool.

948
00:34:19,158 --> 00:34:20,228
It's so cool.

949
00:34:20,758 --> 00:34:22,158
Even if I get rained on,

950
00:34:22,728 --> 00:34:24,898
- my present shouldn't. - What is it?

951
00:34:25,898 --> 00:34:27,828
What?

952
00:34:28,728 --> 00:34:31,088
It stopped raining just in time for us to open the present.

953
00:34:31,438 --> 00:34:33,168
- Right? - Isn't it really fascinating?

954
00:34:34,008 --> 00:34:35,108
Oh, my.

955
00:34:35,638 --> 00:34:37,108
Hurry up and open it.

956
00:34:37,538 --> 00:34:38,938
- Well... - I'm curious too.

957
00:34:40,108 --> 00:34:41,208
What is it?

958
00:34:41,778 --> 00:34:44,548
- This present contains my love. - Okay.

959
00:34:44,748 --> 00:34:46,618
Just think that it contains my love.

960
00:34:47,148 --> 00:34:50,087
You didn't need to bring anything. You worked so hard today.

961
00:34:50,088 --> 00:34:51,987
I didn't expect to receive anything.

962
00:34:51,988 --> 00:34:55,188
- It's nothing big. - What is it?

963
00:34:55,358 --> 00:34:57,028
- Look that way. - What?

964
00:34:57,998 --> 00:34:59,528
Turn around.

965
00:34:59,768 --> 00:35:00,897
- Turn around? - That's right.

966
00:35:00,898 --> 00:35:01,997
Just what did you prepare?

967
00:35:01,998 --> 00:35:03,068
Seriously.

968
00:35:04,738 --> 00:35:06,238
At the same time?

969
00:35:06,668 --> 00:35:08,108
- They need to be together. - I see.

970
00:35:09,168 --> 00:35:10,278
What is it?

971
00:35:12,438 --> 00:35:13,508
It's...

972
00:35:14,878 --> 00:35:16,448
Late at night,

973
00:35:17,348 --> 00:35:18,678
I put in a lot of work.

974
00:35:18,848 --> 00:35:21,948
- What is it? - Take a look. One, two, three.

975
00:35:27,858 --> 00:35:31,098
(She becomes emotional as soon as she sees the present.)

976
00:35:38,498 --> 00:35:40,267
- Oh, my. - Are those paintings?

977
00:35:40,268 --> 00:35:41,467
(Hyun Moo painted Na Rae's grandpa and grandma.)

978
00:35:41,468 --> 00:35:42,908
Your painting skills have improved a lot.

979
00:35:46,208 --> 00:35:47,778
Hyun Moo, you did a good thing.

980
00:35:52,888 --> 00:35:53,988
(Korean passport)

981
00:36:10,598 --> 00:36:12,838
They're your best paintings yet.

982
00:36:14,768 --> 00:36:15,837
Seriously.

983
00:36:15,838 --> 00:36:17,838
(They're your best paintings yet.)

984
00:36:26,518 --> 00:36:28,987
I thought about the past.

985
00:36:28,988 --> 00:36:30,358
(Clearing his throat)

986
00:36:34,688 --> 00:36:37,628
(Seeing Hyun Moo choke up makes her cry even more.)

987
00:36:48,108 --> 00:36:49,588
You should keep them at a nice place.

988
00:36:59,218 --> 00:37:00,948
What's that?

989
00:37:01,418 --> 00:37:03,488
- What's that? - I added these details...

990
00:37:04,388 --> 00:37:07,858
because Grandpa said that he wanted to travel the world.

991
00:37:08,058 --> 00:37:09,327
I made him a passport...

992
00:37:09,328 --> 00:37:10,957
and drew an airplane.

993
00:37:10,958 --> 00:37:13,068
- So that he could travel? - Yes.

994
00:37:13,398 --> 00:37:15,158
It was his dream to travel around the world.

995
00:37:19,038 --> 00:37:20,938
(Grandpa, why do you want a globe?)

996
00:37:21,668 --> 00:37:25,708
(Since I'm too old to travel, I want to do it through the globe.)

997
00:37:26,548 --> 00:37:28,808
(For Grandpa who dreamed of traveling around the world)

998
00:37:29,978 --> 00:37:31,578
It's meaningful.

999
00:37:32,448 --> 00:37:34,547
And when I think about your grandma...

1000
00:37:34,548 --> 00:37:35,787
- You recognize it, right? - Yes.

1001
00:37:35,788 --> 00:37:37,888
Farming chili peppers come to mind first.

1002
00:37:39,928 --> 00:37:42,048
(With Na Rae's grandma who had the brightest smile,)

1003
00:37:43,428 --> 00:37:46,928
(he made unforgettable memories in summer.)

1004
00:37:47,098 --> 00:37:48,567
- I have good memories. - He's a very good painter.

1005
00:37:48,568 --> 00:37:52,598
(She misses her grandma and grandpa.)

1006
00:37:56,708 --> 00:37:58,677
I painted them. I thought...

1007
00:37:58,678 --> 00:38:00,758
it'd be nice to hang them up somewhere in the house.

1008
00:38:00,848 --> 00:38:01,848
I see.

1009
00:38:01,849 --> 00:38:04,078
- Gosh, Hyun Moo. - My grandparents will love them.

1010
00:38:05,348 --> 00:38:06,488
- Really? - Yes.

1011
00:38:07,548 --> 00:38:10,458
I thought about ways to revive the energy of the house.

1012
00:38:10,888 --> 00:38:13,428
While I lack the skills,

1013
00:38:13,888 --> 00:38:16,758
I'm the painter who touches people's hearts.

1014
00:38:16,858 --> 00:38:18,527
- He's saying that about himself. - So...

1015
00:38:18,528 --> 00:38:20,368
- No one did, so I had to. - Today, it's true.

1016
00:38:20,468 --> 00:38:21,567
- It's true. - Yes.

1017
00:38:21,568 --> 00:38:23,938
- Today, it's true. - Yes.

1018
00:38:24,268 --> 00:38:26,738
I took out all the paints I had at home.

1019
00:38:26,968 --> 00:38:29,907
If I had more time, I would've made...

1020
00:38:29,908 --> 00:38:32,977
- bigger paintings with more care. - It's okay.

1021
00:38:32,978 --> 00:38:35,778
But I focused on putting my heart into it.

1022
00:38:36,008 --> 00:38:37,817
Even before I started painting,

1023
00:38:37,818 --> 00:38:39,618
I thought about where to put the heart.

1024
00:38:40,148 --> 00:38:42,887
I added the heart at the very end.

1025
00:38:42,888 --> 00:38:44,017
(I added the heart at the very end after much consideration.)

1026
00:38:44,018 --> 00:38:46,158
In the afterlife, I hope they'll hold hands...

1027
00:38:46,658 --> 00:38:49,028
and make many happy memories together.

1028
00:38:49,658 --> 00:38:51,328
I painted with that thought.

1029
00:38:52,498 --> 00:38:56,768
The thing is, I'm a very rational person.

1030
00:38:58,068 --> 00:38:59,768
I'm not the type to get emotional...

1031
00:38:59,938 --> 00:39:01,608
and shed tears.

1032
00:39:02,508 --> 00:39:03,978
But I got emotional.

1033
00:39:04,378 --> 00:39:06,878
It must be because I'm aging.

1034
00:39:07,248 --> 00:39:10,048
If you give a painting as a present, you usually paint the recipient.

1035
00:39:10,678 --> 00:39:13,118
You don't paint what the recipient misses.

1036
00:39:13,488 --> 00:39:14,918
That was...

1037
00:39:16,788 --> 00:39:18,788
- what I really liked. - I never imagined it.

1038
00:39:19,488 --> 00:39:21,227
(Well...)

1039
00:39:21,228 --> 00:39:24,358
Among Hyun Moo's paintings, they were the best.

1040
00:39:26,498 --> 00:39:27,768
You really are Moosquiat.

1041
00:39:28,598 --> 00:39:30,198
- Now, - Now, he's good.

1042
00:39:30,398 --> 00:39:32,797
He has established his style.

1043
00:39:32,798 --> 00:39:35,608
Seriously, I've said it before.

1044
00:39:36,278 --> 00:39:38,508
They didn't seem like someone else's grandparents.

1045
00:39:38,838 --> 00:39:41,248
I thought about the past and watched the old videos.

1046
00:39:41,708 --> 00:39:43,718
She really looks like my grandma.

1047
00:39:44,378 --> 00:39:45,617
I would've done better...

1048
00:39:45,618 --> 00:39:47,418
- if I had more time. - It's okay.

1049
00:39:48,118 --> 00:39:49,218
I love your present.

1050
00:39:54,658 --> 00:39:56,797
I felt like a member of that family.

1051
00:39:56,798 --> 00:39:59,127
She feels like an actual younger sister now.

1052
00:39:59,128 --> 00:40:01,688
- She really feels that way. - She may be more muscular than me,

1053
00:40:02,068 --> 00:40:04,368
but my heart broke for her throughout the day.

1054
00:40:04,568 --> 00:40:07,008
We spent many years together and went through many hardships.

1055
00:40:07,638 --> 00:40:09,408
We've left many memories together.

1056
00:40:09,678 --> 00:40:11,178
We're a family.

1057
00:40:12,408 --> 00:40:13,784
Hyun Moo is my eldest older brother.

1058
00:40:13,808 --> 00:40:16,548
He embraces us, understands us,

1059
00:40:16,778 --> 00:40:20,117
and watches over us. He gives us good advice too.

1060
00:40:20,118 --> 00:40:22,547
Kian84 is my second eldest older brother.

1061
00:40:22,548 --> 00:40:25,518
There are people who take care of you while grumbling, right?

1062
00:40:25,788 --> 00:40:28,528
They feel just like my older brothers.

1063
00:40:29,828 --> 00:40:31,628
We are like brothers in arms,

1064
00:40:32,358 --> 00:40:34,004
but we never get discharged from the army.

1065
00:40:34,028 --> 00:40:35,588
He said something completely different.

1066
00:40:35,798 --> 00:40:37,698
I lost count with our ranks now.

1067
00:40:38,598 --> 00:40:41,238
We're like brothers in arms who never get discharged.

1068
00:40:41,338 --> 00:40:42,508
We're soldiers for life.

1069
00:40:43,778 --> 00:40:45,377
You're so good. It's making me reflect upon myself.

1070
00:40:45,378 --> 00:40:47,477
- What are you saying? - What? It's raining again!

1071
00:40:47,478 --> 00:40:48,707
(What? It's raining again!)

1072
00:40:48,708 --> 00:40:50,648
- Gosh! - Hurry!

1073
00:40:50,718 --> 00:40:52,248
- Let's put them in here. - Here.

1074
00:40:52,448 --> 00:40:53,847
- Gosh. - It's okay.

1075
00:40:53,848 --> 00:40:55,187
(Rushing in case his work gets wet)

1076
00:40:55,188 --> 00:40:56,418
They can't be rained on.

1077
00:40:57,058 --> 00:40:58,287
Gosh, it's raining all of a sudden.

1078
00:40:58,288 --> 00:40:59,357
I got so surprised.

1079
00:40:59,358 --> 00:41:01,027
It rained all of a sudden...

1080
00:41:01,028 --> 00:41:02,628
- as soon as we looked at them. - Right.

1081
00:41:03,098 --> 00:41:04,498
They must be watching us.

1082
00:41:04,528 --> 00:41:06,628
- My gosh. - How was it timed like that?

1083
00:41:06,968 --> 00:41:08,328
- Gosh! - Na Rae!

1084
00:41:08,668 --> 00:41:10,767
I have a gift for you as well.

1085
00:41:10,768 --> 00:41:11,937
You have a gift for her as well?

1086
00:41:11,938 --> 00:41:13,768
My gift for you is labor.

1087
00:41:14,238 --> 00:41:15,367
That works as a gift.

1088
00:41:15,368 --> 00:41:17,107
- Right. - Kian84.

1089
00:41:17,108 --> 00:41:18,437
I came here to work like a dog.

1090
00:41:18,438 --> 00:41:20,678
Right. You're the best!

1091
00:41:20,878 --> 00:41:22,608
I will make sure to repay the two of you.

1092
00:41:23,918 --> 00:41:25,148
- Gosh. - Gosh.

1093
00:41:25,818 --> 00:41:27,518
It suddenly rained so much.

1094
00:41:28,288 --> 00:41:29,648
Hello!

1095
00:41:30,718 --> 00:41:32,787
- Gosh, everyone went wild... - Gosh.

1096
00:41:32,788 --> 00:41:35,257
After last week's Rainbow Live trailer.

1097
00:41:35,258 --> 00:41:36,657
- Everyone went wild. - Right.

1098
00:41:36,658 --> 00:41:39,697
In my sentimental playlist that I listen to these days,

1099
00:41:39,698 --> 00:41:40,897
I have a lot of this group's songs.

1100
00:41:40,898 --> 00:41:41,898
Really?

1101
00:41:41,899 --> 00:41:43,537
That is why I really wanted to meet this person.

1102
00:41:43,538 --> 00:41:45,737
I think he's the most good-looking drummer that I know.

1103
00:41:45,738 --> 00:41:46,938
- Gosh. - Right.

1104
00:41:47,008 --> 00:41:48,537
For the first time as a Korean band,

1105
00:41:48,538 --> 00:41:50,678
all seats of Gocheok Sky Dome were sold out.

1106
00:41:51,308 --> 00:41:53,248
The driving force of Korea's band boom.

1107
00:41:53,608 --> 00:41:55,777
It's DAY6's drummer, Dowoon!

1108
00:41:55,778 --> 00:41:58,048
(DAY6's drummer, Dowoon)

1109
00:41:59,848 --> 00:42:02,348
(A band boom will come. No. It has already come with DAY6.)

1110
00:42:03,458 --> 00:42:04,757
("Goyang Stadium Sold Out, KSPO DOME's Largest Audience")

1111
00:42:04,758 --> 00:42:06,438
("First K-Band to Enter Gocheok Sky Dome")

1112
00:42:07,558 --> 00:42:09,857
(To the refreshing sound, )

1113
00:42:09,858 --> 00:42:12,028
(the drummer breathes in life.)

1114
00:42:12,998 --> 00:42:14,698
(DAY6, Dowoon)

1115
00:42:15,728 --> 00:42:18,438
- You were beautiful - You were beautiful

1116
00:42:19,098 --> 00:42:21,137
(Bowing)

1117
00:42:21,138 --> 00:42:23,337
- Hello. - Hello.

1118
00:42:23,338 --> 00:42:25,307
He's standing next to me. Gosh, he's handsome.

1119
00:42:25,308 --> 00:42:26,708
- He's handsome. - He's handsome.

1120
00:42:26,908 --> 00:42:29,447
I'm Yoon Dowoon, the one who plays the drums for DAY6.

1121
00:42:29,448 --> 00:42:31,048
- It's a pleasure to meet you. - Yes!

1122
00:42:32,448 --> 00:42:34,347
Can we ask how you feel?

1123
00:42:34,348 --> 00:42:37,417
I've always watched this show on TV.

1124
00:42:37,418 --> 00:42:39,318
I'm feeling nervous to be here myself.

1125
00:42:39,758 --> 00:42:41,087
- He has a strong accent. - Busan?

1126
00:42:41,088 --> 00:42:42,864
- Are you from Gyeongsang Province? - Are you from Busan?

1127
00:42:42,888 --> 00:42:44,627
I'm from the Changwon and Busan area.

1128
00:42:44,628 --> 00:42:46,197
He has a pretty face,

1129
00:42:46,198 --> 00:42:48,468
but he smells like a macho.

1130
00:42:48,598 --> 00:42:50,597
I can tell right away as I'm a macho myself.

1131
00:42:50,598 --> 00:42:52,238
(It's great to see you, my fellow macho.)

1132
00:42:52,398 --> 00:42:55,007
Hasn't it been a while since you've moved to Seoul?

1133
00:42:55,008 --> 00:42:56,877
It has been almost ten years.

1134
00:42:56,878 --> 00:42:59,037
But your accent still feels quite strong.

1135
00:42:59,038 --> 00:43:02,078
I didn't find the need to change my accent.

1136
00:43:02,878 --> 00:43:04,577
He sounds so charming. It's great to hear.

1137
00:43:04,578 --> 00:43:06,247
Gosh, thank you.

1138
00:43:06,248 --> 00:43:08,448
- Don't get rid of your accent. - Got it.

1139
00:43:09,188 --> 00:43:11,087
(A Man's Day)

1140
00:43:11,088 --> 00:43:12,318
"A Man's Day."

1141
00:43:13,628 --> 00:43:15,357
(Rustling)

1142
00:43:15,358 --> 00:43:17,328
(Fascinated)

1143
00:43:18,028 --> 00:43:21,168
Gosh, you keep it so dark as you're a man.

1144
00:43:22,668 --> 00:43:25,298
(Vibrating)

1145
00:43:29,238 --> 00:43:30,378
Hello.

1146
00:43:30,538 --> 00:43:33,608
It is currently 2:40am.

1147
00:43:33,878 --> 00:43:35,747
- It's 2:40am? - It is 19°C.

1148
00:43:35,748 --> 00:43:38,377
- Why wake up at this hour? - The lowest temperature is 16°C.

1149
00:43:38,378 --> 00:43:40,387
- Sean doesn't get up at this hour. - The highest temperature is 27°C.

1150
00:43:40,388 --> 00:43:42,888
He would be fast asleep at this hour.

1151
00:43:43,988 --> 00:43:45,018
Bixby.

1152
00:43:45,618 --> 00:43:47,378
Turn off the air conditioner in the bedroom.

1153
00:43:48,128 --> 00:43:49,827
Okay.

1154
00:43:49,828 --> 00:43:51,798
(He wakes up with Bixby.)

1155
00:43:54,828 --> 00:43:56,568
(Getting up)

1156
00:43:58,238 --> 00:44:02,068
(He walks out.)

1157
00:44:08,878 --> 00:44:10,318
Hello.

1158
00:44:10,448 --> 00:44:12,817
I'm Yoon Dowoon, and I play the drums for DAY6.

1159
00:44:12,818 --> 00:44:15,648
It has been 5 to 6 years since I've lived alone.

1160
00:44:16,148 --> 00:44:18,488
It has been 4 to 5 months...

1161
00:44:18,858 --> 00:44:20,758
since I've lived in this house.

1162
00:44:20,988 --> 00:44:24,158
I'm an outsider when it comes to interior design.

1163
00:44:24,228 --> 00:44:26,497
A person that I know does interior design.

1164
00:44:26,498 --> 00:44:28,467
That was how I got the interior design done...

1165
00:44:28,468 --> 00:44:29,597
for this place.

1166
00:44:29,598 --> 00:44:32,637
I go on a lot of tours abroad.

1167
00:44:32,638 --> 00:44:34,967
- "Tours abroad." - I hated the hotel vibes.

1168
00:44:34,968 --> 00:44:37,437
I just wanted this place to feel like home.

1169
00:44:37,438 --> 00:44:38,998
- "Like home." - A place I can rest in.

1170
00:44:42,978 --> 00:44:43,978
It's nice.

1171
00:44:44,148 --> 00:44:46,817
- It's neat. - I love that it's neat.

1172
00:44:46,818 --> 00:44:49,088
Nice and neat like home.

1173
00:44:49,148 --> 00:44:51,618
A minimalistic design mixed with some wood.

1174
00:44:53,258 --> 00:44:55,557
He must like keeping things minimal.

1175
00:44:55,558 --> 00:44:56,638
(The minimal home of a man)

1176
00:44:57,458 --> 00:44:58,458
(There's just a bed in the bedroom.)

1177
00:44:58,459 --> 00:45:00,198
It does feel quite empty.

1178
00:45:00,828 --> 00:45:01,898
There isn't anything else.

1179
00:45:03,998 --> 00:45:05,237
(The computer room is neat too.)

1180
00:45:05,238 --> 00:45:07,168
It's neat in here too.

1181
00:45:09,068 --> 00:45:11,078
(The kitchen is minimal too.)

1182
00:45:12,738 --> 00:45:14,307
Gosh, there isn't any furniture.

1183
00:45:14,308 --> 00:45:16,078
- It's like a rental home. - It's so neat.

1184
00:45:16,208 --> 00:45:17,218
Really?

1185
00:45:18,118 --> 00:45:22,218
(The dressing room is filled only with clothes.)

1186
00:45:22,688 --> 00:45:25,858
You must not be interested in home accessories.

1187
00:45:25,988 --> 00:45:27,428
I did place a few.

1188
00:45:27,558 --> 00:45:29,327
But they're all small.

1189
00:45:29,328 --> 00:45:30,528
- They're small? - Yes.

1190
00:45:31,258 --> 00:45:33,297
These are aquatic plants.

1191
00:45:33,298 --> 00:45:35,167
- In my previous house, - So you like aquatic plants.

1192
00:45:35,168 --> 00:45:36,967
I didn't get any sunlight.

1193
00:45:36,968 --> 00:45:38,768
I couldn't grow plants as they all died.

1194
00:45:38,868 --> 00:45:41,368
I'm growing a lot of them now with the sun.

1195
00:45:42,168 --> 00:45:43,677
So there were accessories.

1196
00:45:43,678 --> 00:45:45,577
- Right. - They were just small.

1197
00:45:45,578 --> 00:45:47,607
Right. They're small points in the house.

1198
00:45:47,608 --> 00:45:48,648
"Small points."

1199
00:45:49,678 --> 00:45:50,718
How cute.

1200
00:45:51,348 --> 00:45:54,788
(He goes to the kitchen right after getting up.)

1201
00:46:01,458 --> 00:46:02,558
What's going on?

1202
00:46:03,728 --> 00:46:05,427
(Empty)

1203
00:46:05,428 --> 00:46:06,627
That isn't your house, right?

1204
00:46:06,628 --> 00:46:08,167
- It is my house. - It's a rental home, right?

1205
00:46:08,168 --> 00:46:09,527
- Be clear with us. - No. It's my house.

1206
00:46:09,528 --> 00:46:10,597
- Are you sure? - What is that?

1207
00:46:10,598 --> 00:46:14,067
Our band suddenly became popular.

1208
00:46:14,068 --> 00:46:15,767
We had to work every single day.

1209
00:46:15,768 --> 00:46:17,107
"Suddenly became popular?"

1210
00:46:17,108 --> 00:46:19,738
I only ordered food.

1211
00:46:20,108 --> 00:46:21,808
That feels like a refrigerator of a star.

1212
00:46:21,878 --> 00:46:23,077
(Laughing)

1213
00:46:23,078 --> 00:46:24,747
How long has it taken to reach this far?

1214
00:46:24,748 --> 00:46:27,017
- It took us 10 years. - It took you guys 10 years?

1215
00:46:27,018 --> 00:46:28,247
The long-term investment was a success.

1216
00:46:28,248 --> 00:46:29,517
JYP's long-term investment.

1217
00:46:29,518 --> 00:46:32,017
- Bands with handsome members. - Bands with handsome members.

1218
00:46:32,018 --> 00:46:33,657
There were no bands like that for a while.

1219
00:46:33,658 --> 00:46:36,657
- CNBLUE was the last one. - There were none after CNBLUE.

1220
00:46:36,658 --> 00:46:38,258
- Right. - F.T. Island as well.

1221
00:46:38,428 --> 00:46:40,597
- DAY6 was like a band from a drama. - Right.

1222
00:46:40,598 --> 00:46:41,904
- Gosh. - That was how they felt.

1223
00:46:41,928 --> 00:46:43,067
Gosh.

1224
00:46:43,068 --> 00:46:44,937
- That's a great compliment. - After they appeared,

1225
00:46:44,938 --> 00:46:46,567
more bands came to life.

1226
00:46:46,568 --> 00:46:48,108
It's an honor for us.

1227
00:46:51,978 --> 00:46:55,178
(Drinking cold water on an empty stomach)

1228
00:46:59,348 --> 00:47:01,187
It's so neat.

1229
00:47:01,188 --> 00:47:03,148
It's great as it's so neat.

1230
00:47:05,288 --> 00:47:07,588
(Brushing)

1231
00:47:08,628 --> 00:47:11,198
(He brushes his tongue like a man.)

1232
00:47:11,228 --> 00:47:13,127
(Retching)

1233
00:47:13,128 --> 00:47:14,527
It even shows me retching.

1234
00:47:14,528 --> 00:47:16,404
I thought you were going to reach your stomach.

1235
00:47:16,428 --> 00:47:18,898
It's fascinating to watch me live my life.

1236
00:47:20,308 --> 00:47:23,008
(He brushes his teeth and washes his face at once as a man.)

1237
00:47:24,508 --> 00:47:27,508
(He washed his face just with water.)

1238
00:47:29,378 --> 00:47:31,978
(He heads somewhere without applying any lotion.)

1239
00:47:32,748 --> 00:47:34,888
(Opening)

1240
00:47:39,358 --> 00:47:40,528
What is all that?

1241
00:47:41,588 --> 00:47:42,827
They're fishing rods!

1242
00:47:42,828 --> 00:47:44,358
(There was a hidden room?)

1243
00:47:45,858 --> 00:47:48,967
I turned that place into...

1244
00:47:48,968 --> 00:47:51,098
a storage room slash fishing room.

1245
00:47:52,438 --> 00:47:54,007
So he enjoys fishing.

1246
00:47:54,008 --> 00:47:56,667
I see. This is the man's room.

1247
00:47:56,668 --> 00:47:59,107
Gosh, he must love fishing.

1248
00:47:59,108 --> 00:48:00,447
I absolutely love fishing.

1249
00:48:00,448 --> 00:48:02,307
- Gosh. That's his gear? - He seems to be an expert.

1250
00:48:02,308 --> 00:48:03,347
It seems like it's for sea fishing.

1251
00:48:03,348 --> 00:48:04,348
Right.

1252
00:48:04,349 --> 00:48:05,917
- Right. I enjoy jigging. - Sea fishing.

1253
00:48:05,918 --> 00:48:07,817
I've tried sea fishing quite a lot of times.

1254
00:48:07,818 --> 00:48:10,517
I go from time to time with my friends in Incheon.

1255
00:48:10,518 --> 00:48:11,557
- Right. - Right.

1256
00:48:11,558 --> 00:48:13,657
But we usually rent the fishing gear.

1257
00:48:13,658 --> 00:48:15,257
Right. He seems to be an expert.

1258
00:48:15,258 --> 00:48:16,958
He must know someone that is an expert.

1259
00:48:17,798 --> 00:48:19,757
The reason I woke up early...

1260
00:48:19,758 --> 00:48:22,068
was to go fishing.

1261
00:48:22,198 --> 00:48:24,898
I have pride in fishing.

1262
00:48:25,098 --> 00:48:27,368
I'm quite good at fishing.

1263
00:48:27,508 --> 00:48:29,268
- He's a man. - Gosh.

1264
00:48:30,068 --> 00:48:32,707
(The fishing channel that is a must for fisherman)

1265
00:48:32,708 --> 00:48:34,248
(Fishing instinct)

1266
00:48:35,078 --> 00:48:36,307
You were even on the fishing channel?

1267
00:48:36,308 --> 00:48:37,447
I appeared a lot.

1268
00:48:37,448 --> 00:48:40,487
FTV is a channel with experts.

1269
00:48:40,488 --> 00:48:42,017
- Right. - FTV is a channel...

1270
00:48:42,018 --> 00:48:43,987
that is kept on all the time for lovers of fishing.

1271
00:48:43,988 --> 00:48:45,218
Definitely.

1272
00:48:45,588 --> 00:48:49,028
It's a pleasure to be on that channel as a lover of fishing.

1273
00:48:49,328 --> 00:48:52,557
The biggest one that I caught was the 97.5cm yellowtail amberjack.

1274
00:48:52,558 --> 00:48:54,468
(Biggest catch: 97.5cm yellowtail amberjack)

1275
00:48:54,628 --> 00:48:56,768
- That's as big as Na Rae. - Right.

1276
00:48:57,538 --> 00:48:59,568
- There isn't much of a difference. - Right.

1277
00:49:00,508 --> 00:49:01,837
There's a fishing competition?

1278
00:49:01,838 --> 00:49:03,108
- Yes. - He competes as well?

1279
00:49:04,178 --> 00:49:07,848
(The man who loves the sea, Do Taigong)

1280
00:49:09,008 --> 00:49:10,948
To go fishing,

1281
00:49:11,018 --> 00:49:12,848
I've been to Geoje, Tongyeong,

1282
00:49:13,048 --> 00:49:16,688
Wando, and the Yellow Sea. I've been all around the country.

1283
00:49:17,118 --> 00:49:18,557
There's something called big-game fishing.

1284
00:49:18,558 --> 00:49:20,327
There's a rod used for amberjacks.

1285
00:49:20,328 --> 00:49:22,528
- You catch amberjacks too? - There's one for sea bass.

1286
00:49:22,588 --> 00:49:24,527
There's one for catching octopus.

1287
00:49:24,528 --> 00:49:26,527
There's one for webfoot octopus.

1288
00:49:26,528 --> 00:49:28,228
There's one for flatfish.

1289
00:49:28,598 --> 00:49:30,768
There's one for several kinds as well.

1290
00:49:31,098 --> 00:49:33,198
- I think that's about it. - He must love fishing.

1291
00:49:33,838 --> 00:49:35,938
(Opening)

1292
00:49:36,768 --> 00:49:40,208
(Shall I start getting out my fishing gear?)

1293
00:49:41,078 --> 00:49:42,647
(The room is filled with fishing gear.)

1294
00:49:42,648 --> 00:49:44,348
He's doing that at 2:30am.

1295
00:49:44,978 --> 00:49:47,048
What are you doing right now?

1296
00:49:47,148 --> 00:49:51,957
I'm getting out the fishing gear...

1297
00:49:51,958 --> 00:49:53,988
for the type of fish that I will be catching.

1298
00:49:54,128 --> 00:49:55,687
The things that I prepare are different...

1299
00:49:55,688 --> 00:49:57,258
based on what I catch.

1300
00:49:57,728 --> 00:49:59,227
The baits that the fish bite are different...

1301
00:49:59,228 --> 00:50:00,727
based on what kind of fish they are.

1302
00:50:00,728 --> 00:50:01,928
- Right. - Right.

1303
00:50:03,468 --> 00:50:05,398
(I'm done.)

1304
00:50:06,438 --> 00:50:11,578
(The life vest that is essential for boat fishing is packed.)

1305
00:50:14,108 --> 00:50:15,108
(He carefully prepares the rods as well.)

1306
00:50:15,109 --> 00:50:16,429
I have no idea what they all are.

1307
00:50:16,478 --> 00:50:18,417
We wouldn't know if he's that much of an expert.

1308
00:50:18,418 --> 00:50:19,478
I have no idea.

1309
00:50:21,688 --> 00:50:25,918
(He packed a lot of his gear.)

1310
00:50:27,788 --> 00:50:30,398
(It's still dark outside.)

1311
00:50:31,398 --> 00:50:33,798
(The man of the sea heads out from his house.)

1312
00:50:35,028 --> 00:50:37,438
So you wake up earlier than a runner if you go fishing.

1313
00:50:37,838 --> 00:50:39,174
What time did you sleep before waking up?

1314
00:50:39,198 --> 00:50:41,167
I slept at around 8 or 9pm.

1315
00:50:41,168 --> 00:50:42,337
That's enough sleep then.

1316
00:50:42,338 --> 00:50:44,308
That's a decent amount of sleep.

1317
00:50:47,108 --> 00:50:48,178
(Humming)

1318
00:50:49,048 --> 00:50:50,107
It's Sung Si Kyung's song.

1319
00:50:50,108 --> 00:50:51,118
(Sung Si Kyung's song)

1320
00:50:52,918 --> 00:50:54,688
You must go to a motel with that song.

1321
00:50:57,400 --> 00:50:59,169
(Dowoon is driving early in the morning.)

1322
00:50:59,170 --> 00:51:00,240
(Humming)

1323
00:51:01,300 --> 00:51:02,370
It's Sung Si Kyung's song.

1324
00:51:05,110 --> 00:51:06,740
You must go to a motel with that song.

1325
00:51:09,540 --> 00:51:11,140
(You must go to a motel with that song.)

1326
00:51:12,980 --> 00:51:13,980
Don't get the wrong idea.

1327
00:51:13,981 --> 00:51:15,419
He enjoys MA.

1328
00:51:15,420 --> 00:51:17,650
- He enjoys going to motels alone. - I see!

1329
00:51:18,250 --> 00:51:20,020
I got so surprised!

1330
00:51:20,920 --> 00:51:21,920
(He remembered the time he went to a motel alone...)

1331
00:51:21,921 --> 00:51:24,359
(after hearing Sung Si Kyung's song.)

1332
00:51:24,360 --> 00:51:26,089
- Gosh. - What were you thinking?

1333
00:51:26,090 --> 00:51:28,199
- No. - What were you thinking?

1334
00:51:28,200 --> 00:51:29,299
- I'm sorry. - He didn't know.

1335
00:51:29,300 --> 00:51:30,869
- Come on, Dowoon! - He didn't know.

1336
00:51:30,870 --> 00:51:32,400
I didn't expect you to be like that!

1337
00:51:32,500 --> 00:51:33,500
So...

1338
00:51:33,501 --> 00:51:35,299
- Gosh, he's turning red! - Why is your face turning red?

1339
00:51:35,300 --> 00:51:36,399
His face is so red!

1340
00:51:36,400 --> 00:51:38,240
- He's been having naughty thoughts. - Dowoon!

1341
00:51:38,310 --> 00:51:40,950
- His ears are completely red! - Your ears are bleeding right now.

1342
00:51:41,110 --> 00:51:42,809
(Blushing)

1343
00:51:42,810 --> 00:51:44,210
He's out of breath on his own.

1344
00:51:44,810 --> 00:51:46,409
"Is this okay?"

1345
00:51:46,410 --> 00:51:48,480
- Gosh. - "Is this okay?"

1346
00:51:48,750 --> 00:51:50,319
"Is this right?"

1347
00:51:50,320 --> 00:51:51,589
"Is this public television?"

1348
00:51:51,590 --> 00:51:53,620
(Is this public television?)

1349
00:51:55,060 --> 00:51:56,119
Gosh.

1350
00:51:56,120 --> 00:51:58,206
- How cute. That was hilarious. - How innocent of you.

1351
00:51:58,230 --> 00:52:00,360
(The innocent man)

1352
00:52:04,000 --> 00:52:05,900
(He plays the beat with his hands.)

1353
00:52:07,770 --> 00:52:09,369
You're playing to the song.

1354
00:52:09,370 --> 00:52:11,110
Right. It has a good bounce.

1355
00:52:11,270 --> 00:52:12,510
It was perfect to play on.

1356
00:52:14,080 --> 00:52:15,110
Bixby.

1357
00:52:16,510 --> 00:52:17,579
- What? - What?

1358
00:52:17,580 --> 00:52:18,610
Bixby.

1359
00:52:18,810 --> 00:52:19,810
(What?)

1360
00:52:19,811 --> 00:52:21,119
Someone's Bixby turned on.

1361
00:52:21,120 --> 00:52:22,649
It turned on after hearing that?

1362
00:52:22,650 --> 00:52:23,749
- Really? - Really?

1363
00:52:23,750 --> 00:52:24,889
(It turned on after hearing that?)

1364
00:52:24,890 --> 00:52:25,890
It was mine.

1365
00:52:25,891 --> 00:52:27,119
But it was Sung Hwan's Bixby.

1366
00:52:27,120 --> 00:52:29,559
It was Sung Hwan's?

1367
00:52:29,560 --> 00:52:31,759
- I'm sorry. - That's fascinating.

1368
00:52:31,760 --> 00:52:32,830
Hold on.

1369
00:52:33,030 --> 00:52:34,359
Why did yours turn on with Dowoon's voice?

1370
00:52:34,360 --> 00:52:35,859
Right. Our voices...

1371
00:52:35,860 --> 00:52:37,199
- must be similar. - They sound quite similar.

1372
00:52:37,200 --> 00:52:39,030
Does it recognize voice tones?

1373
00:52:40,200 --> 00:52:41,570
Will it not rain today?

1374
00:52:42,040 --> 00:52:43,169
(He heads to his destination while checking the weather.)

1375
00:52:43,170 --> 00:52:45,369
It will not rain today.

1376
00:52:45,370 --> 00:52:47,040
He's heading to Incheon?

1377
00:52:47,140 --> 00:52:48,209
Daebu Island.

1378
00:52:48,210 --> 00:52:49,380
Sihwa Seawall.

1379
00:52:52,750 --> 00:52:54,619
(Buzzing)

1380
00:52:54,620 --> 00:52:56,020
That's the sound of a hair clipper.

1381
00:52:57,120 --> 00:52:59,150
- Gosh. - What a man!

1382
00:52:59,790 --> 00:53:01,590
- That's impressive. - Inside the car?

1383
00:53:01,790 --> 00:53:03,559
He's shaving while driving.

1384
00:53:03,560 --> 00:53:05,090
- He's a true man. - Right.

1385
00:53:09,760 --> 00:53:12,229
(Fishing)

1386
00:53:12,230 --> 00:53:16,970
(Signs can be seen one by one.)

1387
00:53:18,940 --> 00:53:21,810
(He safely reaches his destination.)

1388
00:53:22,610 --> 00:53:24,480
(Let's get going.)

1389
00:53:26,110 --> 00:53:27,119
It's so dark.

1390
00:53:27,120 --> 00:53:28,550
What time was that?

1391
00:53:28,920 --> 00:53:31,419
It must've been around 4:30am to 5am.

1392
00:53:31,420 --> 00:53:33,149
- Gosh, that's so early. - That was why he got up at 2:30am.

1393
00:53:33,150 --> 00:53:34,160
That's amazing.

1394
00:53:34,460 --> 00:53:37,129
Usually, how often did you go fishing?

1395
00:53:37,130 --> 00:53:39,059
- Usually... - When you had more time.

1396
00:53:39,060 --> 00:53:42,160
When I had more time, I went for 3 to 4 days a week.

1397
00:53:42,230 --> 00:53:43,329
I rented a guest house.

1398
00:53:43,330 --> 00:53:45,399
- For 3 to 4 days a week? - That was what I did.

1399
00:53:45,400 --> 00:53:47,099
- You must love fishing. - I think I went for...

1400
00:53:47,100 --> 00:53:48,199
- at least 2 to 3 days a week. - He's serious about fishing.

1401
00:53:48,200 --> 00:53:49,716
- That's amazing. - He's a true lover of fishing.

1402
00:53:49,740 --> 00:53:51,570
It just feels so therapeutic to me.

1403
00:53:51,670 --> 00:53:54,210
You must have plans set as you have more time now.

1404
00:53:54,510 --> 00:53:56,209
I'll be going to Wando the day after tomorrow.

1405
00:53:56,210 --> 00:53:57,350
- Wando? - With whom?

1406
00:53:57,410 --> 00:54:00,180
The guy I was holding the fish with like this.

1407
00:54:00,420 --> 00:54:01,619
Captain Hwang.

1408
00:54:01,620 --> 00:54:03,890
I got close to the people who live there.

1409
00:54:03,990 --> 00:54:06,020
Captain Hwang is a big shot in Wando.

1410
00:54:06,790 --> 00:54:08,120
"Big shot."

1411
00:54:08,620 --> 00:54:11,459
I used to go fishing often with my friends.

1412
00:54:11,460 --> 00:54:13,830
It had been several months since I couldn't go fishing.

1413
00:54:14,030 --> 00:54:16,630
As today was my day off,

1414
00:54:16,830 --> 00:54:20,600
I planned to have a time of rest for myself.

1415
00:54:21,340 --> 00:54:23,770
(He diligently gears himself up.)

1416
00:54:26,070 --> 00:54:29,280
(He walks naturally even in the dark.)

1417
00:54:32,450 --> 00:54:34,220
The place that I went to...

1418
00:54:34,720 --> 00:54:36,319
was Jindu Wharf at the Yellow Sea.

1419
00:54:36,320 --> 00:54:37,420
There are so many boats.

1420
00:54:39,890 --> 00:54:41,219
(It's filled with fishermen early in the morning.)

1421
00:54:41,220 --> 00:54:42,620
There are so many people.

1422
00:54:46,230 --> 00:54:49,200
The species that I will catch today is the webfoot octopus.

1423
00:54:49,700 --> 00:54:50,700
- Webfoot octopus? - Yes.

1424
00:54:50,701 --> 00:54:52,499
- They are in season. - In September.

1425
00:54:52,500 --> 00:54:54,569
They are allowed to be caught in September.

1426
00:54:54,570 --> 00:54:56,199
- Gosh. - That's when they are in season.

1427
00:54:56,200 --> 00:54:58,009
People must've waited for this moment.

1428
00:54:58,010 --> 00:54:59,539
They must be filled with eggs.

1429
00:54:59,540 --> 00:55:01,580
- My mouth is watering already. - They taste great.

1430
00:55:03,010 --> 00:55:04,310
(Drooling)

1431
00:55:04,650 --> 00:55:06,080
I'm actually drooling.

1432
00:55:09,520 --> 00:55:11,049
(The webfoot octopus battle happens at around this time...)

1433
00:55:11,050 --> 00:55:12,590
(at the Yellow Sea.)

1434
00:55:14,560 --> 00:55:17,280
- I heard you could catch over 20. - It's really popular these days.

1435
00:55:18,660 --> 00:55:20,189
(Dowoon enters that battle.)

1436
00:55:20,190 --> 00:55:21,560
That must be exciting!

1437
00:55:23,530 --> 00:55:27,000
Gosh, hello. It's been a while.

1438
00:55:27,100 --> 00:55:29,770
It's a person he knows. It's the man with the boat.

1439
00:55:30,370 --> 00:55:31,470
I was late, wasn't I?

1440
00:55:32,310 --> 00:55:34,610
- It's been quite a while. - Thank you.

1441
00:55:35,340 --> 00:55:37,450
He's cold outside and warm inside.

1442
00:55:38,010 --> 00:55:39,879
(Grumbling)

1443
00:55:39,880 --> 00:55:41,620
"Come here!" This is how he feels.

1444
00:55:41,850 --> 00:55:46,120
I follow him a lot like my father of fishing.

1445
00:55:46,290 --> 00:55:48,589
Captains tend to sound like that.

1446
00:55:48,590 --> 00:55:49,590
Right.

1447
00:55:49,591 --> 00:55:51,789
I got on the captain's boat.

1448
00:55:51,790 --> 00:55:53,089
(Sailing with his father of fishing after a while)

1449
00:55:53,090 --> 00:55:54,130
Thank you.

1450
00:55:54,760 --> 00:55:56,400
I can really see that he loves fishing.

1451
00:55:59,330 --> 00:56:01,170
It's been so long since I've seen you.

1452
00:56:02,370 --> 00:56:03,940
It's the captain's wife.

1453
00:56:05,610 --> 00:56:07,979
(He writes his name on the passenger list.)

1454
00:56:07,980 --> 00:56:09,410
Here you are. Thank you.

1455
00:56:10,750 --> 00:56:13,310
- Do you need a vest? - I'm wearing one right now.

1456
00:56:13,550 --> 00:56:15,649
Vests are the pride of fishermen.

1457
00:56:15,650 --> 00:56:17,366
- You must have your own vest? - It's a must.

1458
00:56:17,390 --> 00:56:18,649
You must have your own vest.

1459
00:56:18,650 --> 00:56:20,389
Why? What goes inside the vest?

1460
00:56:20,390 --> 00:56:21,720
It's not about what goes inside.

1461
00:56:22,020 --> 00:56:24,229
It's saying, "I won't wear the standard vest..."

1462
00:56:24,230 --> 00:56:25,889
"that is on the boat."

1463
00:56:25,890 --> 00:56:27,260
"I won't wear the orange vest!"

1464
00:56:27,300 --> 00:56:28,499
It's like when snowboarders have their own suits.

1465
00:56:28,500 --> 00:56:30,160
Right. That's what it's like.

1466
00:56:31,100 --> 00:56:33,270
(As a seasoned fisherman, )

1467
00:56:34,100 --> 00:56:37,910
(he calmly prepares his gear while whistling.)

1468
00:56:38,970 --> 00:56:42,440
When you get on the boat, you have to get yourself prepared.

1469
00:56:42,610 --> 00:56:45,410
That's basic etiquette for fishing.

1470
00:56:47,880 --> 00:56:49,520
It feels like watching a fishing channel.

1471
00:56:50,350 --> 00:56:52,226
I think of the knotting method that is highly utilized.

1472
00:56:52,250 --> 00:56:55,020
It goes in the ring and gets twirled.

1473
00:56:55,090 --> 00:56:56,489
The two links...

1474
00:56:56,490 --> 00:56:59,589
are twirled around up to four times and pulled.

1475
00:56:59,590 --> 00:57:02,700
The knot is made when the original line is then pulled.

1476
00:57:03,260 --> 00:57:04,600
It's the uni knot.

1477
00:57:04,900 --> 00:57:07,000
It can be used a lot when camping as well.

1478
00:57:10,540 --> 00:57:11,610
I'm done!

1479
00:57:13,980 --> 00:57:16,640
(Shall I get my face ready as well?)

1480
00:57:17,680 --> 00:57:19,710
(Squeezing out the sunscreen)

1481
00:57:22,150 --> 00:57:25,019
(Applying in a tough manner)

1482
00:57:25,020 --> 00:57:26,150
- Gosh. - Gosh.

1483
00:57:26,390 --> 00:57:28,259
We're on the same line.

1484
00:57:28,260 --> 00:57:30,260
(The skincare routine of men)

1485
00:57:31,790 --> 00:57:33,189
- We will set sail. - Okay.

1486
00:57:33,190 --> 00:57:34,230
Get ready.

1487
00:57:34,760 --> 00:57:35,899
(He applies a generous amount of sunscreen before they set sail.)

1488
00:57:35,900 --> 00:57:37,499
- A lot must be applied. - Right.

1489
00:57:37,500 --> 00:57:39,220
- Your skin will eat it up anyway. - Right.

1490
00:57:42,040 --> 00:57:45,139
(Setting sail at 5:53am)

1491
00:57:45,140 --> 00:57:46,310
Even before the sun is out.

1492
00:57:46,610 --> 00:57:48,009
- Gosh. - The sunrise will look nice.

1493
00:57:48,010 --> 00:57:49,130
It feels like a drama scene.

1494
00:57:49,740 --> 00:57:50,910
Doesn't it look stunning?

1495
00:57:52,380 --> 00:57:54,120
I feel very excited.

1496
00:57:55,620 --> 00:57:58,350
I wonder if I can meet my expectations.

1497
00:57:59,020 --> 00:58:00,089
Gosh.

1498
00:58:00,090 --> 00:58:01,419
(Reel in with trust. There will be a bite.)

1499
00:58:01,420 --> 00:58:03,660
"Reel in with trust. There will be a bite."

1500
00:58:04,660 --> 00:58:06,729
He's the man of the sea.

1501
00:58:06,730 --> 00:58:08,900
(The man of sea sails with great passion.)

1502
00:58:11,600 --> 00:58:13,870
When I see the wonders of nature,

1503
00:58:14,140 --> 00:58:17,810
I realize how small I am in this world.

1504
00:58:18,110 --> 00:58:19,769
- "How small I am in this world." - "Small."

1505
00:58:19,770 --> 00:58:21,209
I can feel it from what you're saying.

1506
00:58:21,210 --> 00:58:22,379
Right.

1507
00:58:22,380 --> 00:58:23,709
Many things are insignificant compared to this world.

1508
00:58:23,710 --> 00:58:25,380
- Exactly. For sure. - Right.

1509
00:58:27,580 --> 00:58:30,250
(He goes through his pockets while keeping that in mind.)

1510
00:58:32,590 --> 00:58:34,819
(Let's have a chocolate bar.)

1511
00:58:34,820 --> 00:58:36,360
You need to charge up with sugar.

1512
00:58:36,790 --> 00:58:39,190
- Is it a chocolate bar? - I needed some sugar.

1513
00:58:41,630 --> 00:58:44,470
(A sweet first bite of the day)

1514
00:58:46,170 --> 00:58:47,640
(Dropping)

1515
00:58:48,370 --> 00:58:51,010
(His meal has disappeared in a flash.)

1516
00:58:52,310 --> 00:58:53,439
(Eating after blowing on it)

1517
00:58:53,440 --> 00:58:54,580
It's all right.

1518
00:58:55,410 --> 00:58:56,979
- That's fine. - You won't die.

1519
00:58:56,980 --> 00:58:58,410
It's all right. Boats are clean.

1520
00:58:58,910 --> 00:59:00,580
That's right.

1521
00:59:01,420 --> 00:59:02,579
- When we... - Yes?

1522
00:59:02,580 --> 00:59:03,779
Reach there...

1523
00:59:03,780 --> 00:59:05,990
Today's weather forecast has become bad.

1524
00:59:06,850 --> 00:59:09,220
- I heard it's not going to rain. - Right. It's not.

1525
00:59:09,660 --> 00:59:11,760
Everything else is fine, except for the wind.

1526
00:59:12,490 --> 00:59:13,560
I'll do my best.

1527
00:59:16,960 --> 00:59:19,469
(6:08am, arrived at the first spot)

1528
00:59:19,470 --> 00:59:20,500
We're here.

1529
00:59:21,570 --> 00:59:23,900
(Brushing his hair with determination)

1530
00:59:26,410 --> 00:59:29,640
(Shall I show off my skills?)

1531
00:59:29,980 --> 00:59:31,109
- Dowoon. - Yes?

1532
00:59:31,110 --> 00:59:32,956
How many webfoot octopuses do you want to catch today?

1533
00:59:32,980 --> 00:59:33,980
- Me? - Yes.

1534
00:59:33,981 --> 00:59:35,620
- A hundred? - A hundred?

1535
00:59:36,120 --> 00:59:37,690
At least a hundred?

1536
00:59:38,250 --> 00:59:39,420
Between 100 and 150.

1537
00:59:39,620 --> 00:59:41,489
When I go fishing,

1538
00:59:41,490 --> 00:59:43,690
I like sharing what I catch with people around me.

1539
00:59:44,660 --> 00:59:47,529
"I'll share what I catch with you guys."

1540
00:59:47,530 --> 00:59:48,530
This is the vibe.

1541
00:59:48,531 --> 00:59:50,029
- I feel really proud of it. - No way.

1542
00:59:50,030 --> 00:59:51,346
(He shares the raw fish he caught with his fans too.)

1543
00:59:51,370 --> 00:59:53,330
- You want to eat that, right? - Of course I do.

1544
00:59:53,770 --> 00:59:55,869
Tell me anytime.

1545
00:59:55,870 --> 00:59:57,710
- I'll ask you later. - I'll send it to you.

1546
00:59:57,940 --> 00:59:59,639
We should stay close to Dowoon.

1547
00:59:59,640 --> 01:00:00,739
- I know. - Exactly.

1548
01:00:00,740 --> 01:00:02,509
- Let's stay close. - Let's stay close.

1549
01:00:02,510 --> 01:00:04,179
(A flood of demands to become his friend)

1550
01:00:04,180 --> 01:00:06,749
- Leave the seafood to me. - My goodness!

1551
01:00:06,750 --> 01:00:08,779
- Gosh, we have a seafood brother. - That's right.

1552
01:00:08,780 --> 01:00:09,950
Seafood brother?

1553
01:00:10,320 --> 01:00:11,689
Na Rae, we have a new source.

1554
01:00:11,690 --> 01:00:13,250
- We do. All right. - We do.

1555
01:00:14,590 --> 01:00:18,060
(The seafood brother is getting ready to catch webfoot octopuses.)

1556
01:00:19,590 --> 01:00:21,260
The way I catch webfoot octopus...

1557
01:00:22,300 --> 01:00:24,400
is by lowering a sinker first.

1558
01:00:24,700 --> 01:00:26,170
What's a sinker?

1559
01:00:26,800 --> 01:00:29,340
It's a weight used to make the bait sink to the bottom.

1560
01:00:30,240 --> 01:00:32,910
There's this thing called egi attached to a sinker.

1561
01:00:33,340 --> 01:00:34,939
It looks like a fish,

1562
01:00:34,940 --> 01:00:37,510
but it has hooks attached.

1563
01:00:38,080 --> 01:00:39,810
I have to keep shaking it.

1564
01:00:40,180 --> 01:00:42,420
- I keep shaking it. - Oh, like that?

1565
01:00:42,580 --> 01:00:45,120
It becomes heavy when a webfoot octopus gets on it.

1566
01:00:45,520 --> 01:00:48,590
That's when you pull it and reel the line in.

1567
01:00:48,790 --> 01:00:50,830
Then you'll see a webfoot octopus on it.

1568
01:00:51,130 --> 01:00:53,010
It really looks like a fishing YouTube channel.

1569
01:00:55,160 --> 01:00:58,170
(Finally, it's his first cast.)

1570
01:00:59,500 --> 01:01:01,440
(Putting his leg up relaxedly)

1571
01:01:02,570 --> 01:01:06,010
(Dreaming of a full catch, he focuses on the tips of his fingers.)

1572
01:01:09,080 --> 01:01:10,240
(Grabbing)

1573
01:01:12,910 --> 01:01:14,979
(The fishing line begins to bounce all of a sudden.)

1574
01:01:14,980 --> 01:01:16,080
Is it bending?

1575
01:01:16,850 --> 01:01:18,620
(He reels in right away.)

1576
01:01:18,990 --> 01:01:20,389
- Is it here? - Is it here?

1577
01:01:20,390 --> 01:01:22,030
(He caught one as soon as he first cast?)

1578
01:01:22,960 --> 01:01:25,330
(Reeling in hard)

1579
01:01:29,660 --> 01:01:30,830
Oh, no.

1580
01:01:31,630 --> 01:01:32,790
I don't feel good about this.

1581
01:01:34,500 --> 01:01:36,339
(He reels in the bait awkwardly.)

1582
01:01:36,340 --> 01:01:37,910
The wind was strong that day,

1583
01:01:38,270 --> 01:01:40,039
and the current was strong too.

1584
01:01:40,040 --> 01:01:43,880
It was hard for me to sense the bite.

1585
01:01:45,410 --> 01:01:47,250
(Thinking)

1586
01:01:47,820 --> 01:01:51,050
I think a light sinker would be better. It's too heavy now.

1587
01:01:52,520 --> 01:01:54,459
(In the end, he switches the sinker.)

1588
01:01:54,460 --> 01:01:56,220
(Cutting it with his teeth)

1589
01:01:57,420 --> 01:01:59,990
(Okay, let's try it again.)

1590
01:02:03,860 --> 01:02:07,130
(Super focused with the readjusted fishing rod)

1591
01:02:07,770 --> 01:02:09,540
(Pulling lightly)

1592
01:02:12,110 --> 01:02:13,240
Is it a hit?

1593
01:02:14,510 --> 01:02:15,980
(Smiling)

1594
01:02:16,580 --> 01:02:17,609
(You caught one?)

1595
01:02:17,610 --> 01:02:18,709
Really?

1596
01:02:18,710 --> 01:02:20,410
Gosh, what is this?

1597
01:02:20,550 --> 01:02:21,720
You caught one?

1598
01:02:28,220 --> 01:02:30,419
(Here comes a webfoot octopus.)

1599
01:02:30,420 --> 01:02:31,789
- Goodness. - It's big.

1600
01:02:31,790 --> 01:02:32,890
- It's big. - It's big.

1601
01:02:33,290 --> 01:02:35,759
This one's nice.

1602
01:02:35,760 --> 01:02:36,760
(The new sinker)

1603
01:02:37,700 --> 01:02:38,999
- It's quite big. - It's big.

1604
01:02:39,000 --> 01:02:40,846
(Catching his first webfoot octopus in 10 minutes)

1605
01:02:40,870 --> 01:02:42,400
Put it in the container right away.

1606
01:02:43,100 --> 01:02:44,240
Go on in.

1607
01:02:45,770 --> 01:02:46,910
I'll try one more time.

1608
01:02:48,180 --> 01:02:49,480
(Throwing)

1609
01:02:54,620 --> 01:02:55,720
Nice.

1610
01:02:55,820 --> 01:02:57,749
That's quite cool.

1611
01:02:57,750 --> 01:03:00,350
- It must feel different. - He caught two.

1612
01:03:00,690 --> 01:03:01,759
Killing 2 birds with 1 stone.

1613
01:03:01,760 --> 01:03:03,560
(This time, he catches two at once.)

1614
01:03:05,730 --> 01:03:07,890
(The more confident he gets, the higher his leg goes.)

1615
01:03:09,200 --> 01:03:10,430
He's finally smiling.

1616
01:03:16,670 --> 01:03:17,810
He's having a good catch.

1617
01:03:18,040 --> 01:03:19,239
Gosh, this one's big.

1618
01:03:19,240 --> 01:03:20,370
He's gotten the hang of it.

1619
01:03:21,280 --> 01:03:22,379
Gosh.

1620
01:03:22,380 --> 01:03:24,349
(Smiling)

1621
01:03:24,350 --> 01:03:25,910
My shoulders are shrugging with pride.

1622
01:03:26,910 --> 01:03:29,050
You need to meet a good captain, indeed.

1623
01:03:30,020 --> 01:03:31,720
(Gosh, that punk)

1624
01:03:33,150 --> 01:03:35,060
(Feeling another bite)

1625
01:03:35,160 --> 01:03:36,890
What is this? Why is it so heavy?

1626
01:03:37,960 --> 01:03:39,730
(Super taut)

1627
01:03:40,490 --> 01:03:42,260
Gosh, what is this?

1628
01:03:42,660 --> 01:03:44,400
This doesn't feel like a webfoot octopus.

1629
01:03:44,930 --> 01:03:46,170
Gosh, this one feels good.

1630
01:03:47,230 --> 01:03:48,900
(Expectant)

1631
01:03:50,000 --> 01:03:51,170
Gosh, it's cuttlefish.

1632
01:03:52,610 --> 01:03:53,669
Cuttlefish!

1633
01:03:53,670 --> 01:03:55,179
(The radiant cuttlefish)

1634
01:03:55,180 --> 01:03:56,280
That's rare.

1635
01:03:58,150 --> 01:03:59,279
(Let me go!)

1636
01:03:59,280 --> 01:04:00,409
(Squirting ink)

1637
01:04:00,410 --> 01:04:01,480
What?

1638
01:04:02,420 --> 01:04:04,280
(What is this?)

1639
01:04:05,020 --> 01:04:06,089
Gosh, I should wash it.

1640
01:04:06,090 --> 01:04:07,690
(Calm)

1641
01:04:08,860 --> 01:04:11,230
(Keeping it in the fishing net after washing it)

1642
01:04:12,190 --> 01:04:13,330
This is great!

1643
01:04:13,630 --> 01:04:15,630
I didn't have much experience catching cuttlefish.

1644
01:04:17,200 --> 01:04:19,069
(The man of the sea has conquered cuttlefish as well.)

1645
01:04:19,070 --> 01:04:21,470
(Singing)

1646
01:04:22,500 --> 01:04:23,640
What is this sound?

1647
01:04:24,810 --> 01:04:27,170
(Singing)

1648
01:04:27,370 --> 01:04:29,380
(He keeps repeating "trot" out of the blue.)

1649
01:04:30,480 --> 01:04:32,409
(Here we go.)

1650
01:04:32,410 --> 01:04:33,609
He catches one as soon as he sinks it.

1651
01:04:33,610 --> 01:04:34,970
It's a field of webfoot octopuses.

1652
01:04:36,750 --> 01:04:38,830
(As the drummer of DAY6, he gets into the rhythm...)

1653
01:04:39,220 --> 01:04:40,789
(and sings, "Trot.")

1654
01:04:40,790 --> 01:04:41,790
(Swinging)

1655
01:04:42,720 --> 01:04:44,159
(He catches webfoot octopuses in a row.)

1656
01:04:44,160 --> 01:04:45,290
The sense of rhythm...

1657
01:04:45,930 --> 01:04:47,630
Gosh, this spot feels good.

1658
01:04:49,230 --> 01:04:51,399
- Trot - Why do you keep saying that?

1659
01:04:51,400 --> 01:04:55,140
When you fish, you can't help but keep casting a spell.

1660
01:04:55,400 --> 01:05:00,039
It's like how really thick cheese stretches.

1661
01:05:00,040 --> 01:05:01,516
- That's how it is. - That's a nice comparison.

1662
01:05:01,540 --> 01:05:04,380
I have one too, when I go carp fishing with a float.

1663
01:05:04,650 --> 01:05:07,409
Bite it

1664
01:05:07,410 --> 01:05:09,419
That's just your wish.

1665
01:05:09,420 --> 01:05:11,520
After that... I watched him do it.

1666
01:05:11,820 --> 01:05:13,220
After he goes, "Bite it,"

1667
01:05:13,420 --> 01:05:14,559
he says, "Darn it."

1668
01:05:14,560 --> 01:05:15,660
(Darn it, it didn't bite.)

1669
01:05:16,320 --> 01:05:17,859
"That rude carp."

1670
01:05:17,860 --> 01:05:19,560
(Super sulky)

1671
01:05:20,660 --> 01:05:22,630
(Dowoon, whose spell worked well, )

1672
01:05:23,760 --> 01:05:25,199
(has been fishing webfoot octopus for 3 hours.)

1673
01:05:25,200 --> 01:05:26,299
Three hours?

1674
01:05:26,300 --> 01:05:28,039
I see that it could be fun even if he does the same thing...

1675
01:05:28,040 --> 01:05:29,139
for three hours.

1676
01:05:29,140 --> 01:05:30,670
Yes, it's so much fun.

1677
01:05:32,240 --> 01:05:33,409
(Sea and fishing)

1678
01:05:33,410 --> 01:05:34,510
Gosh, this looks good.

1679
01:05:35,640 --> 01:05:37,580
I keep forgetting that he's a drummer.

1680
01:05:38,010 --> 01:05:39,510
(Laughing)

1681
01:05:41,250 --> 01:05:43,650
(An endless parade of webfoot octopuses)

1682
01:05:44,520 --> 01:05:46,760
- This one looks big. - He could catch a hundred easily.

1683
01:05:48,390 --> 01:05:50,520
(Happy)

1684
01:05:50,860 --> 01:05:52,959
Dowoon, how many have you caught so far?

1685
01:05:52,960 --> 01:05:54,830
I think I've caught about 50.

1686
01:05:54,930 --> 01:05:56,429
(He's caught 50!)

1687
01:05:56,430 --> 01:05:58,400
- He's caught quite a lot. - That's quite a lot.

1688
01:06:00,730 --> 01:06:02,839
(I'm the man of the sea.)

1689
01:06:02,840 --> 01:06:04,939
When I go fishing,

1690
01:06:04,940 --> 01:06:06,340
my mind goes blank.

1691
01:06:07,010 --> 01:06:08,610
I just think, "I should catch that."

1692
01:06:08,810 --> 01:06:11,210
"How should I manage this to catch that?"

1693
01:06:11,510 --> 01:06:13,010
"How high should I lift it?"

1694
01:06:13,280 --> 01:06:14,850
"What baits do others use?"

1695
01:06:15,280 --> 01:06:16,650
"It's quite windy now."

1696
01:06:16,950 --> 01:06:19,150
There are so many things to think about while fishing.

1697
01:06:19,320 --> 01:06:22,520
I think it's been really helpful for my mental health.

1698
01:06:22,590 --> 01:06:24,690
- It helps to clear your mind. - That's right.

1699
01:06:25,230 --> 01:06:27,089
How did you first get into fishing?

1700
01:06:27,090 --> 01:06:30,460
There was a time when I felt anxious.

1701
01:06:30,660 --> 01:06:32,000
I thought I should empty my mind.

1702
01:06:33,300 --> 01:06:35,270
The members were going through a hard time,

1703
01:06:35,400 --> 01:06:38,139
so we had to pause our activities for a while.

1704
01:06:38,140 --> 01:06:39,970
There was nothing for me to do.

1705
01:06:40,140 --> 01:06:43,610
I'm a drummer, right? I liked being the drummer of DAY6.

1706
01:06:44,240 --> 01:06:45,779
Nothing could fill the void...

1707
01:06:45,780 --> 01:06:47,309
- no matter what I did. - Of course."

1708
01:06:47,310 --> 01:06:49,680
But fishing helped me fill that void in a healthy way.

1709
01:06:50,520 --> 01:06:52,096
- You got through it nicely. - You did it in a healthy way.

1710
01:06:52,120 --> 01:06:53,590
- It's a good hobby. - Yes.

1711
01:06:55,190 --> 01:06:56,360
Five hours.

1712
01:06:57,690 --> 01:06:58,989
- Dowoon. - Yes?

1713
01:06:58,990 --> 01:07:00,930
- We should eat. - Shall we eat?

1714
01:07:01,430 --> 01:07:02,559
You should make ramyeon.

1715
01:07:02,560 --> 01:07:04,329
- All right. - Let's add webfoot octopuses.

1716
01:07:04,330 --> 01:07:05,799
You should have webfoot octopus ramyeon before you go.

1717
01:07:05,800 --> 01:07:06,900
All right.

1718
01:07:07,170 --> 01:07:09,399
- That's right! - Gosh, that sounds wonderful.

1719
01:07:09,400 --> 01:07:11,610
- Ramyeon. - Ramyeon!

1720
01:07:13,940 --> 01:07:16,510
(Choosing webfoot octopuses to put into ramyeon)

1721
01:07:18,810 --> 01:07:21,480
- There were a lot at the bottom. - Yes.

1722
01:07:21,920 --> 01:07:23,880
(I'm going to eat a lot.)

1723
01:07:24,850 --> 01:07:27,720
Captain. Aren't you going to eat?

1724
01:07:28,290 --> 01:07:30,530
Would you like to try some? Shall I share some with you?

1725
01:07:30,790 --> 01:07:31,930
No, we...

1726
01:07:32,330 --> 01:07:34,130
are going to have meat.

1727
01:07:35,160 --> 01:07:36,329
He's firm.

1728
01:07:36,330 --> 01:07:38,870
People who work at the sea have meat.

1729
01:07:40,100 --> 01:07:41,169
It's all set.

1730
01:07:41,170 --> 01:07:42,770
(It's a shame, but I'll enjoy it alone.)

1731
01:07:42,940 --> 01:07:43,999
Here we go.

1732
01:07:44,000 --> 01:07:45,640
Yes, you should only blanch it.

1733
01:07:47,440 --> 01:07:48,879
- Goodness. - You should have it now.

1734
01:07:48,880 --> 01:07:50,479
- You should have it now. - Eat it when it's slightly cooked.

1735
01:07:50,480 --> 01:07:51,610
- It's now. - Very slightly.

1736
01:07:56,780 --> 01:07:58,119
You can have a webfoot octopus like that?

1737
01:07:58,120 --> 01:07:59,190
Of course.

1738
01:08:00,250 --> 01:08:02,090
(Surprised)

1739
01:08:05,530 --> 01:08:07,030
(Exclaiming)

1740
01:08:13,100 --> 01:08:14,529
Gosh, the ink bursts out.

1741
01:08:14,530 --> 01:08:16,070
Right. There's ink in it.

1742
01:08:18,470 --> 01:08:19,970
My mouth must be all black.

1743
01:08:23,040 --> 01:08:25,080
(Smiling)

1744
01:08:25,410 --> 01:08:26,550
Oh, it's fine.

1745
01:08:27,680 --> 01:08:30,020
It feels like I'm watching a young man from Gyeongsang.

1746
01:08:31,950 --> 01:08:34,310
(The young man from Gyeongsang doesn't give in to the ink.)

1747
01:08:35,320 --> 01:08:36,919
(This is torturing.)

1748
01:08:36,920 --> 01:08:37,990
Goodness.

1749
01:08:38,690 --> 01:08:40,189
- The ink burst. - Gosh.

1750
01:08:40,190 --> 01:08:41,300
It's ink ramyeon.

1751
01:08:43,460 --> 01:08:45,330
(Satisfied)

1752
01:08:50,370 --> 01:08:52,539
- That's romantic. - It's really romantic.

1753
01:08:52,540 --> 01:08:55,680
(Taking a break, using the bow as his bed)

1754
01:08:57,810 --> 01:08:58,880
(Getting up)

1755
01:09:00,480 --> 01:09:02,750
(The webfoot octopus hunt hasn't ended.)

1756
01:09:03,780 --> 01:09:04,790
Gosh.

1757
01:09:06,790 --> 01:09:08,290
He looks so happy.

1758
01:09:10,520 --> 01:09:13,490
(A full catch of webfoot octopus)

1759
01:09:15,160 --> 01:09:17,900
I think I did pretty well today.

1760
01:09:18,300 --> 01:09:21,400
My fishing skills are still sharp.

1761
01:09:21,940 --> 01:09:24,139
The captain kept saying the weather forecast was bad.

1762
01:09:24,140 --> 01:09:26,840
But I feel like I achieved something after catching a lot.

1763
01:09:27,540 --> 01:09:29,780
I think I'm a true fisherman.

1764
01:09:30,940 --> 01:09:33,079
Is your dream to become a fisherman?

1765
01:09:33,080 --> 01:09:36,380
I'm thinking of keeping it as a side job.

1766
01:09:36,520 --> 01:09:38,420
Your final goal is becoming a captain?

1767
01:09:38,520 --> 01:09:40,949
Which do you like more, talking about fishing or music?

1768
01:09:40,950 --> 01:09:43,160
I like both.

1769
01:09:43,190 --> 01:09:44,789
I find both music and fishing fun.

1770
01:09:44,790 --> 01:09:46,589
It's 50-50. It's like how cooking is to me.

1771
01:09:46,590 --> 01:09:47,829
- Seriously. - It must be 50-50.

1772
01:09:47,830 --> 01:09:49,860
The level of his question is crazy high.

1773
01:09:50,200 --> 01:09:51,520
Because he loves fishing so much.

1774
01:09:52,530 --> 01:09:54,969
It's like asking if he likes his mom or dad more.

1775
01:09:54,970 --> 01:09:57,176
You wouldn't have encountered a question like this if it weren't for me.

1776
01:09:57,200 --> 01:09:58,610
(Honored)

1777
01:09:59,610 --> 01:10:01,109
- Great work. - Great job.

1778
01:10:01,110 --> 01:10:02,140
Yes.

1779
01:10:03,680 --> 01:10:05,510
(Current time: 1:49pm)

1780
01:10:05,650 --> 01:10:08,250
(A fishing journey that went on over 8 hours comes to an end.)

1781
01:10:08,720 --> 01:10:10,780
It lasts really long.

1782
01:10:11,750 --> 01:10:13,390
Fishing uses the whole day.

1783
01:10:13,720 --> 01:10:15,120
Gosh, I caught a lot.

1784
01:10:16,620 --> 01:10:19,460
- He caught a lot. - He did, indeed.

1785
01:10:20,660 --> 01:10:21,960
Why is this so big?

1786
01:10:22,960 --> 01:10:24,030
Fifteen.

1787
01:10:24,200 --> 01:10:26,129
- Gosh. - Gosh, wild cuttlefish.

1788
01:10:26,130 --> 01:10:27,430
I'd want that.

1789
01:10:31,070 --> 01:10:33,209
- Excluding the ones you ate... - I caught a lot.

1790
01:10:33,210 --> 01:10:34,670
Excluding them, I caught 70.

1791
01:10:35,640 --> 01:10:37,009
You'd eat that for three days.

1792
01:10:37,010 --> 01:10:38,080
(Filled)

1793
01:10:39,150 --> 01:10:40,749
- I want him. - Seriously.

1794
01:10:40,750 --> 01:10:41,919
He's such a good friend.

1795
01:10:41,920 --> 01:10:43,119
Count on me.

1796
01:10:43,120 --> 01:10:44,819
- What do you need? Count on me. - Webfoot octopus would be good.

1797
01:10:44,820 --> 01:10:46,290
- We need him. - Seriously.

1798
01:10:46,720 --> 01:10:48,259
- I'll get going now. - It was so windy.

1799
01:10:48,260 --> 01:10:49,660
- Great job. - Gosh.

1800
01:10:49,790 --> 01:10:50,890
Bye, Captain.

1801
01:10:51,090 --> 01:10:53,230
Great work.

1802
01:10:54,230 --> 01:10:56,530
(Returning after a full catch)

1803
01:11:01,270 --> 01:11:04,040
Captain, I'll get going!

1804
01:11:04,570 --> 01:11:05,570
He's cool.

1805
01:11:05,571 --> 01:11:07,110
He's the captain I like.

1806
01:11:12,250 --> 01:11:14,749
Fishing like that makes you relieve your stress, right?

1807
01:11:14,750 --> 01:11:16,320
It really does.

1808
01:11:16,480 --> 01:11:18,850
You must feel proud since you caught something.

1809
01:11:20,220 --> 01:11:23,020
(Getting in the car after 9 hours after relieving stress)

1810
01:11:25,830 --> 01:11:29,460
(He sets off without a break.)

1811
01:11:33,330 --> 01:11:35,470
- Hey, Dowoon. - Mom.

1812
01:11:35,600 --> 01:11:36,669
Yes.

1813
01:11:36,670 --> 01:11:39,669
It's just that I went fishing today,

1814
01:11:39,670 --> 01:11:41,009
and I caught a lot of webfoot octopuses,

1815
01:11:41,010 --> 01:11:42,310
so I want to leave them at home.

1816
01:11:42,580 --> 01:11:44,639
- All right. - Okay.

1817
01:11:44,640 --> 01:11:45,810
Bye.

1818
01:11:45,850 --> 01:11:47,980
Your mother rarely uses a dialect.

1819
01:11:48,820 --> 01:11:51,589
It's really strong sometimes,

1820
01:11:51,590 --> 01:11:53,290
but she didn't use much that day.

1821
01:11:55,660 --> 01:11:57,390
(After a call with his mom, )

1822
01:11:58,690 --> 01:12:01,390
(he drives on endlessly.)

1823
01:12:02,330 --> 01:12:03,829
What time was it?

1824
01:12:03,830 --> 01:12:06,729
That was around 3 to 4pm.

1825
01:12:06,730 --> 01:12:07,899
- Gosh. - The day is long.

1826
01:12:07,900 --> 01:12:09,069
It is long, indeed.

1827
01:12:09,070 --> 01:12:10,630
(He's been awake for 12 hours already.)

1828
01:12:11,870 --> 01:12:13,440
(Whistling)

1829
01:12:16,980 --> 01:12:18,420
- That's your parents' house. - Yes.

1830
01:12:19,380 --> 01:12:22,050
(He arrives at his parents' place after driving for a long time.)

1831
01:12:23,980 --> 01:12:25,120
Tori.

1832
01:12:25,990 --> 01:12:27,119
- A chicken? - They must have a dog.

1833
01:12:27,120 --> 01:12:28,260
That's right.

1834
01:12:28,820 --> 01:12:30,860
(He enters as he calls for his dog, Tori.)

1835
01:12:31,860 --> 01:12:32,860
(A cozy living room)

1836
01:12:32,861 --> 01:12:34,489
- It's a parent's home. - The vibe of a parents' home.

1837
01:12:34,490 --> 01:12:35,759
Gosh, the vibe of a parents' home.

1838
01:12:35,760 --> 01:12:36,999
(Pictures that give off the vibe of a parents' home)

1839
01:12:37,000 --> 01:12:39,070
"Fishing in People." Gosh.

1840
01:12:40,470 --> 01:12:41,599
(A kitchen that feels friendly even at a glance)

1841
01:12:41,600 --> 01:12:43,640
It gives off the parents' home vibe a lot.

1842
01:12:44,240 --> 01:12:45,869
(He arrives at his parents' place at 4:11pm.)

1843
01:12:45,870 --> 01:12:47,270
I love that clock.

1844
01:12:48,640 --> 01:12:51,280
(He quickly goes to the kitchen with the webfoot octopus.)

1845
01:12:52,880 --> 01:12:54,709
You have to wash off the ink?

1846
01:12:54,710 --> 01:12:57,120
There was a lot, so I removed it.

1847
01:12:57,320 --> 01:12:59,450
(This will do.)

1848
01:13:01,120 --> 01:13:04,760
My parents love seafood,

1849
01:13:04,990 --> 01:13:06,889
so when I go fishing,

1850
01:13:06,890 --> 01:13:08,300
I'd send what I caught to them.

1851
01:13:08,760 --> 01:13:11,869
I tend to give the seafood to them.

1852
01:13:11,870 --> 01:13:13,700
They must love it.

1853
01:13:14,770 --> 01:13:15,999
(He fills his parents' fridge instead of his.)

1854
01:13:16,000 --> 01:13:18,139
The fridge at your parents' place is always full.

1855
01:13:18,140 --> 01:13:19,670
That's how a fridge should look.

1856
01:13:21,110 --> 01:13:22,139
Tori.

1857
01:13:22,140 --> 01:13:23,860
- Your dog doesn't jump out? - Where is it?

1858
01:13:24,010 --> 01:13:26,910
(It just stays on the bed even when he calls it.)

1859
01:13:27,780 --> 01:13:29,150
You couldn't hear?

1860
01:13:29,280 --> 01:13:30,449
Oh, it must be old.

1861
01:13:30,450 --> 01:13:31,749
- It must be old. - Goodness.

1862
01:13:31,750 --> 01:13:32,789
How old is it?

1863
01:13:32,790 --> 01:13:33,819
It's 16 years old.

1864
01:13:33,820 --> 01:13:35,460
(Tori, 16 years old)

1865
01:13:35,820 --> 01:13:37,619
It's pretty healthy.

1866
01:13:37,620 --> 01:13:38,960
Live a long life.

1867
01:13:39,860 --> 01:13:41,190
Let's take medicine.

1868
01:13:41,900 --> 01:13:44,259
Gosh, there's some left.

1869
01:13:44,260 --> 01:13:45,629
Goodness.

1870
01:13:45,630 --> 01:13:46,930
Are you done?

1871
01:13:47,870 --> 01:13:49,800
(Filled with love)

1872
01:13:52,970 --> 01:13:53,970
(Grunting)

1873
01:13:54,610 --> 01:13:55,680
What?

1874
01:13:56,840 --> 01:13:57,939
They have a cat too.

1875
01:13:57,940 --> 01:13:59,710
- That's a cat? - Yes.

1876
01:14:00,310 --> 01:14:02,319
(He spends a cozy time with Hosooni as well.)

1877
01:14:02,320 --> 01:14:03,780
(Smiling)

1878
01:14:04,880 --> 01:14:06,649
(Jumping)

1879
01:14:06,650 --> 01:14:07,890
Look how strong it is.

1880
01:14:08,460 --> 01:14:10,420
(Bitter)

1881
01:14:11,420 --> 01:14:13,090
(Sulky)

1882
01:14:13,530 --> 01:14:14,929
You can't help it with cats.

1883
01:14:14,930 --> 01:14:16,360
Cats are rude.

1884
01:14:17,430 --> 01:14:19,500
I'll get going.

1885
01:14:19,900 --> 01:14:21,870
Seriously. They're wild.

1886
01:14:23,270 --> 01:14:26,110
(A man who got kicked out by Hosooni)

1887
01:14:27,640 --> 01:14:28,909
(Feeling lonely, he heads somewhere.)

1888
01:14:28,910 --> 01:14:30,380
He must be going somewhere else.

1889
01:14:30,610 --> 01:14:32,080
Please go home and get some rest.

1890
01:14:33,580 --> 01:14:34,910
He's going to Mangwon-dong.

1891
01:14:35,450 --> 01:14:36,680
Is he going fishing again?

1892
01:14:36,780 --> 01:14:38,389
- Oh, fishing by the Han River! - Fishing by the Han River!

1893
01:14:38,390 --> 01:14:39,450
Come on.

1894
01:14:41,150 --> 01:14:42,960
His day is longer than Sean's.

1895
01:14:44,790 --> 01:14:47,230
(He enters a basement parking lot instead of the Han River.)

1896
01:14:50,600 --> 01:14:51,600
(Trudging with the remaining webfoot octopus)

1897
01:14:51,601 --> 01:14:53,170
He must be giving it to someone.

1898
01:14:53,470 --> 01:14:54,710
Is he giving it to his members?

1899
01:14:56,340 --> 01:14:58,840
(Looking around)

1900
01:14:59,510 --> 01:15:01,140
(Pressing)

1901
01:15:01,780 --> 01:15:03,880
- Is it his member? - Maybe we'll see Young K?

1902
01:15:06,680 --> 01:15:07,909
- Sung Woo. - You're here.

1903
01:15:07,910 --> 01:15:11,349
(Sung Woo)

1904
01:15:11,350 --> 01:15:12,790
We haven't seen him for a long time.

1905
01:15:13,720 --> 01:15:17,560
(The legend of the band scene and Dowoon's respected senior)

1906
01:15:19,630 --> 01:15:21,629
- Yes, it's Sung Woo. - Sung Woo.

1907
01:15:21,630 --> 01:15:23,299
It's my respected senior, Sung Woo.

1908
01:15:23,300 --> 01:15:25,129
I went to the house of Nobrain's Sung Woo.

1909
01:15:25,130 --> 01:15:27,130
- So you're close to Sung Woo. - Yes.

1910
01:15:27,670 --> 01:15:29,399
He's a grateful person.

1911
01:15:29,400 --> 01:15:30,799
He's the senior I respect the most.

1912
01:15:30,800 --> 01:15:33,910
Even when we weren't that famous,

1913
01:15:34,210 --> 01:15:36,209
he'd call us out to eat. "Dowoon, what are you doing?"

1914
01:15:36,210 --> 01:15:39,579
"I'm having a meal. Do you want to join?"

1915
01:15:39,580 --> 01:15:40,909
It's really nice to see them together.

1916
01:15:40,910 --> 01:15:43,519
He always takes care of us.

1917
01:15:43,520 --> 01:15:46,690
He'd be like, "Dowoon, don't you need a leather jacket?"

1918
01:15:46,920 --> 01:15:49,090
I aspire to be like him the exact way he is.

1919
01:15:49,290 --> 01:15:51,990
I want to become a senior like him.

1920
01:15:52,460 --> 01:15:55,030
- Hey, Dowoon. - Are you busy, Sung Woo?

1921
01:15:55,160 --> 01:15:56,299
No, I'm not.

1922
01:15:56,300 --> 01:15:59,929
It's just that I caught a lot of webfoot octopuses.

1923
01:15:59,930 --> 01:16:02,169
Yes. You don't have to bring that for me.

1924
01:16:02,170 --> 01:16:04,240
Come on, Sung Woo.

1925
01:16:05,040 --> 01:16:06,109
Thank you.

1926
01:16:06,110 --> 01:16:07,909
- All right, Sung Woo. - Okay.

1927
01:16:07,910 --> 01:16:09,639
You must've given a lot of stuff before.

1928
01:16:09,640 --> 01:16:11,779
Yes, I'd give him yellowtail...

1929
01:16:11,780 --> 01:16:13,579
whenever I catch them.

1930
01:16:13,580 --> 01:16:15,619
Oh, dear. Now you have to drop by...

1931
01:16:15,620 --> 01:16:17,519
- Itaewon and Hannam-dong too. - I know.

1932
01:16:17,520 --> 01:16:18,649
- Of course. - Sure.

1933
01:16:18,650 --> 01:16:20,170
Why don't you just get a water tanker?

1934
01:16:21,250 --> 01:16:22,359
He's right. And sell them.

1935
01:16:22,360 --> 01:16:23,519
- That's a good idea. - Yes.

1936
01:16:23,520 --> 01:16:24,689
I'll buy you a water tanker.

1937
01:16:24,690 --> 01:16:26,029
That sounds good!

1938
01:16:26,030 --> 01:16:28,700
- He should, seriously. - You'd get me a water tanker?

1939
01:16:28,830 --> 01:16:30,700
- Can I come in? - Yes, come on in.

1940
01:16:31,200 --> 01:16:32,929
He hasn't changed at all.

1941
01:16:32,930 --> 01:16:34,769
- Let's go in. - I caught this.

1942
01:16:34,770 --> 01:16:36,339
- Yes. - It's a lot, though.

1943
01:16:36,340 --> 01:16:37,470
Goodness.

1944
01:16:37,670 --> 01:16:39,170
Do you run a shop or something?

1945
01:16:39,710 --> 01:16:41,310
Should I really?

1946
01:16:41,840 --> 01:16:43,080
Come on in.

1947
01:16:44,310 --> 01:16:45,609
Is he also from Busan?

1948
01:16:45,610 --> 01:16:46,850
Sung Woo is from Masan.

1949
01:16:47,780 --> 01:16:48,919
(The Gyeongsang men use a strong dialect...)

1950
01:16:48,920 --> 01:16:50,240
(as soon as they see each other.)

1951
01:16:50,280 --> 01:16:51,790
Is this your newlywed home?

1952
01:16:52,220 --> 01:16:53,819
It'll become one.

1953
01:16:53,820 --> 01:16:55,020
It will?

1954
01:16:56,020 --> 01:16:57,420
Right, that's how it was.

1955
01:16:59,030 --> 01:17:00,729
- Goodness. - It's so neat.

1956
01:17:00,730 --> 01:17:02,136
("Nobrain's Lee Sung Woo is Getting Married in October")

1957
01:17:02,160 --> 01:17:04,100
Is it okay for me to come in?

1958
01:17:05,800 --> 01:17:06,870
- How cute. - Don't bark.

1959
01:17:07,300 --> 01:17:09,069
- Dubu, be quiet. - How old is Dubu?

1960
01:17:09,070 --> 01:17:10,540
Dubu is 13.

1961
01:17:10,600 --> 01:17:12,170
It's the same age as Comme Des.

1962
01:17:12,970 --> 01:17:14,710
Can I just put it in the fridge?

1963
01:17:14,770 --> 01:17:17,679
I think you should...

1964
01:17:17,680 --> 01:17:20,410
wash the insides and remove all the ink first.

1965
01:17:20,680 --> 01:17:22,379
I can just put it in the fridge for now, right?

1966
01:17:22,380 --> 01:17:23,850
Yes, you can.

1967
01:17:24,420 --> 01:17:28,119
How long did you fish for that you caught this much?

1968
01:17:28,120 --> 01:17:30,320
You know I got skills.

1969
01:17:30,420 --> 01:17:32,659
This would cost a lot.

1970
01:17:32,660 --> 01:17:33,830
He seems really happy.

1971
01:17:34,030 --> 01:17:35,130
It wouldn't cost a lot.

1972
01:17:35,630 --> 01:17:37,659
Wild webfoot octopus must be expensive.

1973
01:17:37,660 --> 01:17:38,876
(He's grateful for the gift Dowoon brings every time.)

1974
01:17:38,900 --> 01:17:40,629
- Goodness, Sung Woo. - Have you eaten?

1975
01:17:40,630 --> 01:17:42,139
Eat? I think I'm going to fall asleep.

1976
01:17:42,140 --> 01:17:43,500
He must be tired.

1977
01:17:43,800 --> 01:17:45,160
You don't have anyone to eat with?

1978
01:17:45,310 --> 01:17:47,009
I don't really like leaving the house.

1979
01:17:47,010 --> 01:17:48,779
Really? But you're out already.

1980
01:17:48,780 --> 01:17:50,509
I don't really go out if it isn't for fishing.

1981
01:17:50,510 --> 01:17:51,510
I went out to fish.

1982
01:17:51,511 --> 01:17:53,510
Was it yellowtail?

1983
01:17:53,610 --> 01:17:55,196
- Oh, right. - Yellowtail and sea bream.

1984
01:17:55,220 --> 01:17:56,879
Once, you sent them...

1985
01:17:56,880 --> 01:17:59,820
and told me, "It smells like it's going to be spoiled soon,"

1986
01:17:59,950 --> 01:18:02,160
"so quickly grill it and eat it." So I grilled it.

1987
01:18:02,820 --> 01:18:04,020
(Feeling proud)

1988
01:18:04,390 --> 01:18:06,230
Don't you two have quite an age gap?

1989
01:18:06,360 --> 01:18:09,300
- How many years apart are you two? - He's 19 years older than me.

1990
01:18:10,030 --> 01:18:11,729
- But Sung Woo is... - He lives young.

1991
01:18:11,730 --> 01:18:13,830
Don't you feel uncomfortable?

1992
01:18:13,870 --> 01:18:16,839
- I'm not particular about it. - That's nice.

1993
01:18:16,840 --> 01:18:18,469
- It's not that I'm rude. - No.

1994
01:18:18,470 --> 01:18:21,109
I don't really mind the age.

1995
01:18:21,110 --> 01:18:22,539
Older guys like that better.

1996
01:18:22,540 --> 01:18:24,179
- Yes, thank you so much. - That's right.

1997
01:18:24,180 --> 01:18:25,949
If you get along well, age doesn't matter.

1998
01:18:25,950 --> 01:18:27,210
It really doesn't matter.

1999
01:18:27,510 --> 01:18:28,679
You'll come to my wedding, right?

2000
01:18:28,680 --> 01:18:31,179
Oh, right. Of course, I will.

2001
01:18:31,180 --> 01:18:32,919
If you don't come after saying that you will...

2002
01:18:32,920 --> 01:18:34,920
I emptied my schedule. These days...

2003
01:18:36,190 --> 01:18:38,189
I'll give you my wedding invitation.

2004
01:18:38,190 --> 01:18:40,260
- Really? - My wedding invitation.

2005
01:18:40,960 --> 01:18:42,530
A wedding invitation.

2006
01:18:42,630 --> 01:18:46,770
I'll treasure it like a family treasure.

2007
01:18:48,200 --> 01:18:49,770
(Solemn)

2008
01:18:50,500 --> 01:18:52,900
(A man who gives up his knees upon receiving the invitation)

2009
01:18:53,010 --> 01:18:54,770
Who takes a wedding invitation on his knees?

2010
01:18:56,810 --> 01:18:59,450
- My wife-to-be... - Yes?

2011
01:18:59,680 --> 01:19:02,620
Designed everything and put the sticker... Oh, she didn't.

2012
01:19:03,520 --> 01:19:04,549
Here.

2013
01:19:04,550 --> 01:19:05,550
(Readily taking it)

2014
01:19:06,190 --> 01:19:08,319
- The man I admire the most. - Don't do that.

2015
01:19:08,320 --> 01:19:09,320
(Embarrassed)

2016
01:19:09,620 --> 01:19:11,220
Try not showing up and see what happens.

2017
01:19:11,320 --> 01:19:13,480
I'll write your name down on my death note right away.

2018
01:19:14,160 --> 01:19:15,599
You should open it, though.

2019
01:19:15,600 --> 01:19:18,329
- Gosh, it's... - Open it! Your senior...

2020
01:19:18,330 --> 01:19:20,499
- I wanted to save it for later. - You're not even opening it?

2021
01:19:20,500 --> 01:19:21,700
I wanted to save it for later.

2022
01:19:22,140 --> 01:19:23,839
(Impressed)

2023
01:19:23,840 --> 01:19:24,870
I knew it.

2024
01:19:25,610 --> 01:19:27,410
(How is it?)

2025
01:19:29,510 --> 01:19:32,280
- "After a long wait..." - Stop it.

2026
01:19:33,280 --> 01:19:34,850
"After a long wait..."

2027
01:19:34,950 --> 01:19:36,819
(Stop!)

2028
01:19:36,820 --> 01:19:38,889
- "The two of us..." - No.

2029
01:19:38,890 --> 01:19:41,019
(The man from Gyeongsang can't tolerate awkwardness.)

2030
01:19:41,020 --> 01:19:43,920
Don't use it as a ramyeon coaster.

2031
01:19:44,490 --> 01:19:46,930
- It does like people. - It's really funny.

2032
01:19:47,590 --> 01:19:48,900
It likes you.

2033
01:19:49,560 --> 01:19:51,399
It likes when its butt is patted.

2034
01:19:51,400 --> 01:19:54,169
It once caused a sensation...

2035
01:19:54,170 --> 01:19:55,840
- It was popular? - as a singing dog.

2036
01:19:56,270 --> 01:19:57,440
It can sing?

2037
01:19:58,870 --> 01:20:01,039
(Dubu was once the talk of the town as a singing dog.)

2038
01:20:01,040 --> 01:20:02,910
- That's right. - You're so good.

2039
01:20:03,280 --> 01:20:05,580
(The father is proud.)

2040
01:20:06,210 --> 01:20:08,310
There are a few songs that Dubu likes.

2041
01:20:09,150 --> 01:20:10,820
One of them is...

2042
01:20:11,850 --> 01:20:14,650
(Playing Dubu's favorite song)

2043
01:20:16,360 --> 01:20:17,789
- Look at its face. - It's getting ready.

2044
01:20:17,790 --> 01:20:18,830
Stand by.

2045
01:20:19,990 --> 01:20:21,359
(Could it actually sing?)

2046
01:20:21,360 --> 01:20:22,360
Does it still sing?

2047
